#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""glaeda-gh: one shared GitHub CI watcher for every agent session on this machine.

    glaeda-gh serve                                     the daemon (LaunchAgent or systemd user unit)
    glaeda-gh status pr OWNER/REPO#N [--json]           PR state, checks, mergeability, review
    glaeda-gh status run OWNER/REPO/RUN_ID [--jobs] [--json]
    glaeda-gh wait pr OWNER/REPO#N [--until green|done|required|merged] [--timeout S] [--json]
    glaeda-gh wait run OWNER/REPO/RUN_ID [--jobs] [--rescue-grace S] [--timeout S] [--json]
    glaeda-gh wait comment OWNER/REPO#N [--author LOGIN] [--match REGEX] [--since ID|URL|TIME|10m]
    glaeda-gh status comment OWNER/REPO#N               the latest comments on an issue or PR
    glaeda-gh budget [--json]                           REST and GraphQL quota as last seen

Many sessions share one GitHub account and so one REST quota (5000 requests an hour). Each
session polling its own PRs and runs used it up while the separate GraphQL quota sat idle. Here
the daemon is the only process that talks to GitHub, and clients only read its cache:

- Clients register interest by writing a small file in the watch directory; its mtime is the
  last-interest time. An entry nobody has read for 15 minutes is dropped, and a merged, closed or
  completed one 2 minutes after its last reader.
- Every watched PR is read in batched GraphQL queries each cycle (60 s), including the rate limit.
  GitHub answers 504 to a query that runs past ~10 s, so a batch starts at 10 PRs, halves on a 504
  or timeout and retries the halves at once, and grows back by one after 5 clean queries. A PR
  that times out alone is read alone until it answers. `budget` shows the batch size and the
  oldest data of an open watch.
  A check that failed gets its failed steps from one more small query, once: a completed check
  does not change.
- Runs use REST with If-None-Match, so an unchanged run answers 304, which GitHub does not count
  against the quota. The job list is read when a client passed --jobs, and once per attempt when
  a run fails, for the failed steps.
- REST and GraphQL are one pool. Each tick, PR reads and run reads each go to whichever bucket is
  richer for the time it has left: what remains above its reserve divided by the seconds until
  its own reset (600 left with 2 minutes to go is rich; 600 left with 50 is poor). A kind moves
  only when the other bucket is SWITCH_MARGIN richer, so it does not flap, and both buckets drain
  about evenly. Runs over GraphQL are one aliased `resource(url:)` query per batch of runs (job lists
  stay on REST: only REST names the runner, which tells a refusal from a failure). PRs over REST
  read the pull, its head's check runs, statuses and workflow runs, the workflow names, and the
  base branch's required checks, every one with If-None-Match; REST has no review decision, so the last one
  GraphQL read is kept. Comment watches stay on GraphQL.
- Comment watches (an issue's or PR's latest 50 comments) are one batched GraphQL query every 15 s,
  and last only while someone waits on them.
- The X-RateLimit headers and the GraphQL rateLimit field drive a budget guard: below the floor
  (500 left) the daemon polls four times less often and skips job lists; below the reserve (100
  left) it stops that API until the quota resets. The quota is shared, so this also protects
  every other tool on the account.
- The token comes from `gh auth token` and stays in memory; it is never written or logged.
- The cmux build controller on the tailnet (cmuxterm-hq#675) receives GitHub's workflow_run,
  check_suite and issue_comment webhooks. Each tick the daemon reads its change feed once: free,
  so a finished run shows up within about TICK seconds and costs no quota, a check event on a
  watched PR's head commit or branch re-reads that PR at once (at most every NUDGE_MIN seconds),
  and a new comment reaches comment waits. While the controller tracks a run, REST reads it only
  as a safety net every 15 minutes; when the controller is unreachable or has never seen the run,
  the REST path above applies unchanged. Public repositories need no token; private ones need the
  controller token. Controller: $CMUX_CI_CONTROLLER, else the cmux-ci client config, else the
  fleet default; token: $CMUX_CI_TOKEN_FILE, else ~/.config/cmux/build-fleet/controller.token.

Clients make no API calls. When the daemon is not running they say so and exit 3 rather than
fall back to polling. `wait` exits 0 on success (or a matching comment), 1 on failure (a failed
check, a failed run, a PR closed without being merged, an issue that does not exist), 2 on timeout,
4 when the PR conflicts with its base. --until green (default) fails as soon as any completed check
failed, required or not, and succeeds once every check finished with success, neutral or skipped;
done waits for every check to finish; required lets only the checks GitHub requires decide (when
any are marked); merged waits for the merge.

A PR that conflicts with its base (mergeable CONFLICTING, merge state DIRTY), for example after the
PR it was stacked on was squash-merged, runs no pull_request workflows until the next push. `wait
pr --until green|done|required` therefore stops with exit 4 as soon as the daemon's cached data says so;
--until merged keeps waiting. UNKNOWN mergeability (GitHub computes it lazily) counts as pending.

A job a glaeda runner refused (its "Set up runner" step, the runner's job-started hook, failed
within six minutes; a GUI job may wait up to four for the gui token first) is usually re-run
elsewhere by the repository's own rescue. `wait` holds such a failure as pending for
--rescue-grace seconds (default 360) and follows the next attempt; a failure with any other
cause decides at once.

State lives in ~/.cache/glaeda/gh (GLAEDA_GH_DIR overrides): watch/, cache/, budget.json,
daemon.json (a heartbeat and the last hour's request counts) and daemon.lock, which the daemon
holds for its lifetime and names itself in. Every GitHub request has a hard deadline (HTTP_DEADLINE),
every subprocess one too, and a tick starts no request after TICK_BUDGET (what it did not reach goes
first next tick). A watchdog thread exits the daemon with EXIT_WATCHDOG, printing every thread's
stack to stderr, when its loop has not come round for WATCHDOG_AFTER, and launchd or systemd starts
a new one. A daemon waiting for the lock stops a holder silent for STALE_HEARTBEAT (a live glaeda-gh
serve, checked first) and takes over. `serve` refuses to start from a shell while the LaunchAgent or
systemd unit is loaded, unless given --foreground-ok: sessions never run their own. A cycle that raises still writes the heartbeat, with the error's type and
code site under "failing"; once cycles have failed for FAILING_AFTER seconds, clients exit 3 and
name the error instead of waiting on a daemon that refreshes nothing. The daemon re-executes itself when this file changes, so an update takes
effect without a restart.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import contextlib
import datetime as dt
import faulthandler
import fcntl
import hashlib
from http.client import HTTPException  # the http() helper below shadows the `http` package
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import traceback
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any, Callable

BASE = Path(os.environ.get("GLAEDA_GH_DIR") or Path.home() / ".cache/glaeda/gh")
API = "https://api.github.com"
CYCLE = 60  # seconds between polls of a live PR or run
COMMENT_CYCLE = 15  # comment watches: someone is waiting for a reply, and one batched query costs ~1 point
TICK = 2  # how often the daemon looks for new interest; a new entry is filled within one tick
IDLE_EXPIRY = 15 * 60  # drop an entry nobody has read for this long
TERMINAL_GRACE = 120  # drop a merged, closed or completed entry (or a comment watch) this long after its last reader
TERMINAL_REFRESH = 600  # a terminal entry still watched is re-read this rarely (a rerun reopens a run)
CACHE_KEEP = 3600  # unwatched cache files are removed after this long
LEAN_FACTOR = 4  # below the floor, poll this many times less often
FLOORS = {"rest": (500, 100), "graphql": (500, 100)}  # (floor, reserve) per quota
GQL_BATCH = 15  # most PRs (or runs, or comment watches) per GraphQL query
# GitHub answers 504 when a query runs past ~10 s. Measured 2026-10-19 on cosmic-gray: 25 PRs per
# query took 10-11 s (half answered 504), 10 took 4-6 s, 5 took 2-3 s; each cost 1 point. So each
# kind's batch starts at GQL_BATCH_START, halves on a 504 or timeout, and grows by one after
# GQL_GROW_AFTER clean queries in a row.
GQL_BATCH_START = 10
GQL_GROW_AFTER = 5
GQL_SPLITS = 8  # queries a kind may add per tick by splitting timed-out batches
GQL_BATCH_MIN = 2  # each query costs a point however small, so the batch never drops below this
SLOW = re.compile(r"HTTP 504|[Tt]imeout|timed out")  # the query took too long: shrink and split
FLAKY = re.compile(r"HTTP 50[23]")  # GitHub hiccuped: split and retry, but the size was not the cause
HEAVY_AFTER = 3  # lone timeouts in a row after which a heavy entry is read only every LEAN_FACTOR cycles
MAX_BODY = 8 * 1024 * 1024
WAIT_POLL = 3  # clients re-read the cache this often
TOUCH_EVERY = 30  # clients refresh their interest this often
DOWN_GRACE = 5.0  # clients look this long for the daemon before calling it down (it re-executes on update)
HEARTBEAT_EVERY = 30  # the daemon rewrites daemon.json at least this often
# A request's socket timeout bounds each read, not the request: a server that trickles bytes, or a tick
# of many slow reads, kept one 10-20 daemon silent for 20 minutes. So every request also has a hard
# wall-clock deadline, a tick stops starting requests after TICK_BUDGET (the rest go first next tick),
# and a watchdog thread exits the process when the loop has not come round for WATCHDOG_AFTER, so
# launchd or systemd starts a fresh one.
SOCKET_TIMEOUT = 20  # each connect, TLS handshake and read
HTTP_DEADLINE = 45  # one whole GitHub request, from connect to the last body byte
SUBPROCESS_DEADLINE = 20  # gh auth token, launchctl, ps: killed with their whole process group after
TICK_BUDGET = 90  # seconds a tick may spend starting requests
WATCHDOG_CYCLES = 5
WATCHDOG_AFTER = WATCHDOG_CYCLES * CYCLE  # the loop silent this long is hung: exit EXIT_WATCHDOG
EXIT_WATCHDOG = 75  # EX_TEMPFAIL: the service manager restarts the daemon
# The watchdog thread logs before it exits, so a main thread stuck inside a write to stdout or stderr
# (a full pipe, a hung disk) holds the stream lock it needs. faulthandler's own C thread needs neither
# the GIL nor that lock: it ends the process (status 1) this long after the watchdog should have.
WATCHDOG_BACKSTOP = 60
STALE_HEARTBEAT = 2 * WATCHDOG_AFTER  # a lock holder silent this long is hung; a waiting daemon takes over
LOCK_RETRY = 15  # a daemon waiting for the lock looks at its holder this often
TAKEOVER_GRACE = 10  # after SIGTERM, how long a hung holder has before SIGKILL
FAILING_AFTER = 120  # every tick raising for this long is a broken daemon, not a transient error
FAILED = {"FAILURE", "TIMED_OUT", "CANCELLED", "ACTION_REQUIRED", "STARTUP_FAILURE", "ERROR", "STALE"}
STEP_FAILED = {"FAILURE", "TIMED_OUT", "STARTUP_FAILURE", "ACTION_REQUIRED"}  # a cancelled step is not the cause
PASSED = {"SUCCESS", "NEUTRAL", "SKIPPED"}
RUN_OK = {"success", "neutral", "skipped"}
# The runner's job-started hook shows as this step. glaeda's hook fails it when the host refuses the
# job (busy, draining); the repository's rescue (cmux: owned_pool_rescue.py) then re-runs the job.
HOOK_STEP = "Set up runner"
# a refusal fails at job start, after up to 240 s waiting for the gui token (cmux owned_pool_rescue's window)
REFUSAL_SECONDS = 360
RESCUE_GRACE = 360  # how long a refused attempt stays pending while a rescue re-run may follow
GLAEDA_RUNNER = re.compile(r"(?:^|-)glaeda(?:-|$)")
COMMENTS_LAST = 50  # comments per issue per read: more than this within one 15 s cycle are missed
COMMENT_BODY_MAX = 4000
STEPS_BATCH = 50  # check runs per steps query
EXIT_OK, EXIT_FAIL, EXIT_TIMEOUT, EXIT_DOWN, EXIT_CONFLICT = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4
CONTROLLER_DEFAULT = "http://100.89.225.106:18765"  # cmux-lawrence since the 2026-09-28 move
CONTROLLER_CONFIG = Path.home() / ".local/share/cmux-fleet-client/client.json"
CONTROLLER_TOKEN = Path.home() / ".config/cmux/build-fleet/controller.token"
CONTROLLER_TIMEOUT = 3  # a tick must not stall on an unreachable controller
CONTROLLER_DEADLINE = 6  # one whole controller request
CONTROLLER_FEED_EVERY = 20  # seconds between change-feed reads: each request costs the controller a tailnet
# whois, and 18 hosts at one read per tick drove tailscaled into a kernel panic on cmux-lawrence (10-07)
CONTROLLER_RETRY = 60  # after a failure, the controller is left alone this long, doubling up to CONTROLLER_ABSENT
REST_SAFETY = 900  # a run the controller tracks is still read over REST this often (a lost delivery)
CONTROLLER_ABSENT = 900  # a controller without the run-events receiver (404, 503) is asked again this rarely
FEED_PAGES = 5  # feed pages read per tick at most; a longer backlog continues on the next tick
NUDGE_MIN = 20  # a PR a check event nudged is re-read at most this often (GraphQL points)

NAME = r"[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+"
PR_RE = re.compile(rf"(?:https://github\.com/)?({NAME})/({NAME})(?:#|/pull/)(\d+)(?:[/?#].*)?")
RUN_RE = re.compile(rf"(?:https://github\.com/)?({NAME})/({NAME})/(?:actions/runs/)?(\d+)(?:[/?#].*)?")
ISSUE_RE = re.compile(rf"(?:https://github\.com/)?({NAME})/({NAME})(?:#|/pull/|/issues/)(\d+)(?:[/?#].*)?")
KEY_RES = {"pr": PR_RE, "run": RUN_RE, "comment": ISSUE_RE}
CYCLES = {"pr": CYCLE, "run": CYCLE, "comment": COMMENT_CYCLE}
BUCKETS = {"pr": "graphql", "run": "rest", "comment": "graphql"}  # each kind's usual bucket
OTHER = {"rest": "graphql", "graphql": "rest"}
BALANCED = ("pr", "run")  # kinds either bucket can read; comment watches stay on GraphQL
RICH_WINDOW_MIN = 60  # seconds: a bucket about to reset is rich, but not infinitely so
FULL_WINDOW = 3600  # a bucket not seen yet, or past its reset, counts as a full hour's quota
SWITCH_MARGIN = 1.25  # a kind moves to the other bucket only when that one is this much richer
REST_CHECK_PAGES = 5  # check-run pages (100 each) one PR read over REST takes at most
REST_STEPS_MAX = 10  # failed or cancelled jobs whose steps one PR read over REST looks up at most

# NUM is the alias's PR-number variable: isRequired needs it
PR_FIELDS = """number url title state isDraft merged mergedAt mergeable mergeStateStatus headRefOid
headRefName reviewDecision updatedAt baseRef { branchProtectionRule { requiredStatusCheckContexts }
rules(first: 50) { nodes { type parameters { ... on RequiredStatusChecksParameters {
  requiredStatusChecks { context } } } } } } commits(last: 1) { nodes { commit { statusCheckRollup { state
contexts(first: 100) { totalCount pageInfo { hasNextPage } nodes { __typename
... on CheckRun { id name status conclusion detailsUrl databaseId startedAt completedAt
  isRequired(pullRequestNumber: NUM) checkSuite { conclusion workflowRun { databaseId workflow { name databaseId } } } }
... on StatusContext { context state targetUrl createdAt isRequired(pullRequestNumber: NUM) } } } } } } }"""
COMMENT_FIELDS = f"""comments(last: {COMMENTS_LAST}) {{ totalCount nodes {{ databaseId url createdAt
author {{ login }} body }} }}"""
ISSUE_FIELDS = f"""__typename ... on Issue {{ url state {COMMENT_FIELDS} }}
... on PullRequest {{ url state {COMMENT_FIELDS} }}"""
RUN_FIELDS = """__typename ... on WorkflowRun { databaseId runAttempt event createdAt updatedAt url displayTitle
workflow { name } checkSuite { status conclusion commit { oid } branch { name } } }"""
STEPS_QUERY = """query($ids: [ID!]!) { rateLimit { limit remaining resetAt cost }
nodes(ids: $ids) { ... on CheckRun { id steps(first: 100) { nodes { name conclusion } } } } }"""
TOKEN_RE = re.compile(r"(gh[pousr]_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,251}|github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,251}|[0-9a-f]{40})")


class WatchError(RuntimeError):
    pass


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- keys and files


def parse_key(kind: str, text: str) -> str:
    """`pr:owner/repo#n`, `run:owner/repo/id` or `comment:owner/repo#n`, lowercased so every session
    shares one entry."""
    match = KEY_RES[kind].fullmatch(text.strip()) if kind in KEY_RES else None
    if not match:
        form = {"pr": "OWNER/REPO#N or a pull request URL", "run": "OWNER/REPO/RUN_ID or a run URL",
                "comment": "OWNER/REPO#N or an issue or pull request URL"}.get(kind, "pr, run or comment")
        raise WatchError(f"{kind} must be {form}, not {text!r}")
    owner, repo, number = match.groups()
    sep = "/" if kind == "run" else "#"
    return f"{kind}:{owner.lower()}/{repo.lower()}{sep}{int(number)}"


def split_key(key: str) -> tuple[str, str, str, int]:
    kind, rest = key.split(":", 1)
    owner, rest = rest.split("/", 1)
    repo, number = re.split(r"[#/]", rest, maxsplit=1)
    return kind, owner, repo, int(number)


def watch_dir() -> Path:
    return BASE / "watch"


def cache_dir() -> Path:
    return BASE / "cache"


def file_name(key: str) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(key.encode()).hexdigest()[:32] + ".json"


def write_json(path: Path, doc: Any) -> None:
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True, mode=0o700)
    tmp = path.with_name(f".{path.name}.{os.getpid()}.{time.monotonic_ns()}.tmp")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(doc, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def read_json(path: Path) -> Any:
    try:
        return json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def read_cache(key: str) -> dict | None:
    doc = read_json(cache_dir() / file_name(key))
    return doc if isinstance(doc, dict) and doc.get("key") == key else None


def register(key: str, jobs: bool = False) -> None:
    """Record interest in `key`. Wanting jobs is sticky while any reader wants them."""
    path = watch_dir() / file_name(key)
    prior = read_json(path) or {}
    write_json(path, {"key": key, "jobs": bool(jobs or prior.get("jobs"))})


def terminal(kind: str, data: dict | None) -> bool:
    """Merged or closed PRs and completed runs; a comment watch never is (a reply can always come)."""
    if not data or kind == "comment":
        return False
    return data.get("state") in {"MERGED", "CLOSED"} if kind == "pr" else data.get("status") == "completed"


def iso_epoch(text: str | None) -> float | None:
    try:
        return dt.datetime.fromisoformat(str(text).replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except ValueError:
        return None


def quick(started: str | None, completed: str | None) -> bool:
    """Finished within REFUSAL_SECONDS of starting: a job-start refusal, not a job that ran."""
    start, end = iso_epoch(started), iso_epoch(completed)
    return start is not None and end is not None and end - start <= REFUSAL_SECONDS


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- GitHub transport


class NoRedirect(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):
    """urllib would copy the Authorization header to wherever a redirect points; refuse them all."""

    def redirect_request(self, req, fp, code, msg, headers, newurl):  # noqa: ANN001
        return None


OPENER = urllib.request.build_opener(NoRedirect)


def error_site(error: BaseException) -> str:
    """Where `error` was raised: file, line and function only, never values, so a recurring error
    can be found without reproducing it."""
    frames = traceback.extract_tb(error.__traceback__)
    return f"{Path(frames[-1].filename).name}:{frames[-1].lineno} {frames[-1].name}" if frames else ""


def describe(error: BaseException) -> str:
    """Our own messages are safe to show; anything else is reduced to its type name."""
    return str(error) if isinstance(error, WatchError) else type(error).__name__


class DeadlineTimeout(TimeoutError):
    """A hard deadline passed (hard_deadline). A TimeoutError, so callers treat it as a slow answer."""


@contextlib.contextmanager
def hard_deadline(seconds: float):
    """Raise DeadlineTimeout in the block once `seconds` of wall-clock time pass, even in the middle of a
    blocking read: a socket timeout only bounds each read, so a server that trickles bytes never
    trips it. Uses SIGALRM, so only on the main thread (the daemon's loop); elsewhere it does
    nothing and the watchdog still applies."""
    if threading.current_thread() is not threading.main_thread() or not hasattr(signal, "setitimer"):
        yield
        return

    def expire(signum: int, frame: Any) -> None:
        raise DeadlineTimeout(f"no complete answer within {seconds:g}s")

    previous = signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, expire)
    signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, seconds)
    try:
        yield
    finally:
        signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, 0)
        signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, previous)


def http(method: str, url: str, headers: dict[str, str], body: bytes | None = None) -> tuple[int, dict, bytes]:
    if not url.startswith(API + "/"):
        raise WatchError("refusing a request outside api.github.com")
    request = urllib.request.Request(url, data=body, method=method, headers=headers)
    try:
        with hard_deadline(HTTP_DEADLINE):
            try:
                with OPENER.open(request, timeout=SOCKET_TIMEOUT) as response:
                    return response.status, {k.lower(): v for k, v in response.headers.items()}, response.read(MAX_BODY)
            except urllib.error.HTTPError as error:  # 304, refused redirects and every 4xx/5xx land here
                return error.code, {k.lower(): v for k, v in (error.headers or {}).items()}, error.read(MAX_BODY)
    except (OSError, HTTPException, ValueError) as error:  # DeadlineTimeout is a TimeoutError, an OSError
        raise WatchError(f"request failed: {type(error).__name__}") from None


def run_bounded(argv: list[str], timeout: float | None = None) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """subprocess.run(capture_output=True, text=True) with a deadline that holds. On a timeout run()
    kills only the child and then waits for its pipes, which a grandchild that inherited them keeps
    open for as long as it lives. Here the child leads its own process group and the whole group is
    killed. Raises subprocess.TimeoutExpired or OSError."""
    timeout = SUBPROCESS_DEADLINE if timeout is None else timeout
    with subprocess.Popen(argv, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
                          text=True, start_new_session=True) as proc:
        try:
            out, err = proc.communicate(timeout=timeout)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
            with contextlib.suppress(OSError):  # a child that left its group; Popen.__exit__ waits for it
                proc.kill()
            with contextlib.suppress(subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError, ValueError):
                proc.communicate(timeout=2)
            for stream in (proc.stdout, proc.stderr):  # a grandchild that left the group still holds them
                with contextlib.suppress(OSError):
                    stream.close()
            raise subprocess.TimeoutExpired(argv, timeout) from None
    return subprocess.CompletedProcess(argv, proc.returncode, out, err)


def find_gh() -> str | None:
    """gh on PATH, else where Homebrew and distributions put it: a LaunchAgent's PATH is minimal."""
    for candidate in (shutil.which("gh"), os.fspath(Path.home() / ".local/bin/gh"), "/opt/homebrew/bin/gh",
                      "/usr/local/bin/gh", "/usr/bin/gh"):
        if candidate and os.access(candidate, os.X_OK):
            return candidate
    return None


def gh_token() -> str:
    gh = find_gh()
    if gh is None:
        raise WatchError("gh not found")
    try:
        result = run_bounded([gh, "auth", "token", "--hostname", "github.com"])
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as error:
        raise WatchError(f"gh auth token: {type(error).__name__}") from None
    token = result.stdout.strip()
    if result.returncode != 0 or not token:  # neither stream is repeated: stdout may be a token
        raise WatchError(f"gh auth token exited {result.returncode}; is gh signed in?")
    if not TOKEN_RE.fullmatch(token):
        raise WatchError("gh auth token printed something that is not a GitHub token")
    return token


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- cmux build controller


class OutOfTime(Exception):
    """This tick spent TICK_BUDGET: the entries it did not reach go first on the next tick. Not a
    WatchError, so no reader stores it as an entry's error."""


class NoReceiver(WatchError):
    """The controller answers but serves no run events (not deployed there, or not configured)."""


class Controller:
    """The build controller's GitHub events (cmuxterm-hq#675) over the tailnet: no GitHub quota.
    Without a token it answers for public repositories only."""

    def __init__(self, base: str, token: str | None) -> None:
        self.base, self.token = base.rstrip("/"), token

    @classmethod
    def from_env(cls) -> "Controller | None":
        base = os.environ.get("CMUX_CI_CONTROLLER", "").strip()
        if not base:
            doc = read_json(CONTROLLER_CONFIG)
            base = (doc.get("controller") if isinstance(doc, dict) else None) or CONTROLLER_DEFAULT
        if not isinstance(base, str) or not base.startswith(("http://", "https://")):
            return None
        path = os.environ.get("CMUX_CI_TOKEN_FILE") or os.fspath(CONTROLLER_TOKEN)
        try:
            token = Path(path).read_text().strip() or None
        except (OSError, ValueError):  # ValueError: a token file that is not UTF-8
            token = None
        return cls(base, token)

    def get(self, path: str) -> dict:
        """One JSON answer. Raises NoReceiver (404, 503) or WatchError."""
        headers = {"Authorization": f"Bearer {self.token}"} if self.token else {}
        request = urllib.request.Request(self.base + path, headers=headers)
        try:
            with hard_deadline(CONTROLLER_DEADLINE), OPENER.open(request, timeout=CONTROLLER_TIMEOUT) as response:
                doc = json.loads(response.read(MAX_BODY))
        except urllib.error.HTTPError as error:
            if error.code in {404, 503}:
                raise NoReceiver(f"controller HTTP {error.code}") from None
            raise WatchError(f"controller HTTP {error.code}") from None
        except (OSError, HTTPException, ValueError) as error:
            raise WatchError(f"controller: {type(error).__name__}") from None
        if not isinstance(doc, dict):
            raise WatchError("controller: not a JSON object")
        return doc

    def run(self, run_id: int) -> dict | None:
        """The controller's view of a run, or None when it has never seen it. Raises WatchError."""
        doc = self.get(f"/v1/github/runs/{int(run_id)}?wait=0")
        run = doc.get("run")
        return run if doc.get("known") and isinstance(run, dict) else None

    def events(self, since: int | None) -> dict:
        """The change feed after cursor `since`, or only the current cursor when since is None."""
        doc = self.get("/v1/github/events?since=" + ("latest" if since is None else str(int(since))))
        if not isinstance(doc.get("cursor"), int) or doc["cursor"] < 0:
            raise WatchError("controller: feed without a cursor")
        return doc


def controller_run(run: dict) -> dict:
    """The controller's run record in slim_run's shape."""
    return {"id": run.get("id"), "name": run.get("name"), "display_title": None, "status": run.get("status"),
            "conclusion": run.get("conclusion") or None, "html_url": run.get("url"), "head_sha": run.get("head_sha"),
            "head_branch": run.get("head_branch") or "", "event": run.get("event"), "run_attempt": run.get("attempt"),
            "created_at": None, "updated_at": run.get("updated_at")}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- GraphQL for PRs


def pr_query(keys: list[str], kind: str = "pr") -> tuple[str, dict]:
    """One aliased query for every PR (or issue's comments) in `keys`, plus the rate limit. Names
    travel as variables. PRs are aliased p0, p1, ...; comment watches c0, c1, ..."""
    decls, parts, variables = [], [], {}
    for i, key in enumerate(keys):
        _, owner, repo, number = split_key(key)
        decls.append(f"$o{i}: String!, $r{i}: String!, $n{i}: Int!")
        if kind == "pr":
            fields = PR_FIELDS.replace("NUM", f"$n{i}")
            parts.append(f"p{i}: repository(owner: $o{i}, name: $r{i}) {{ pullRequest(number: $n{i}) {{ {fields} }} }}")
        else:
            parts.append(f"c{i}: repository(owner: $o{i}, name: $r{i}) {{ issueOrPullRequest(number: $n{i}) "
                         f"{{ {ISSUE_FIELDS} }} }}")
        variables.update({f"o{i}": owner, f"r{i}": repo, f"n{i}": number})
    query = f"query({', '.join(decls)}) {{ rateLimit {{ limit remaining resetAt cost }} {' '.join(parts)} }}"
    return query, variables


def run_query(keys: list[str]) -> tuple[str, dict]:
    """One aliased query for every run in `keys` (w0, w1, ...), by URL, plus the rate limit."""
    decls, parts, variables = [], [], {}
    for i, key in enumerate(keys):
        _, owner, repo, run_id = split_key(key)
        decls.append(f"$u{i}: URI!")
        parts.append(f"w{i}: resource(url: $u{i}) {{ {RUN_FIELDS} }}")
        variables[f"u{i}"] = f"https://github.com/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs/{run_id}"
    query = f"query({', '.join(decls)}) {{ rateLimit {{ limit remaining resetAt cost }} {' '.join(parts)} }}"
    return query, variables


def graphql_run(node: dict) -> dict:
    """A WorkflowRun node in slim_run's shape (REST's lowercase status and conclusion)."""
    suite = node.get("checkSuite") or {}
    return {"id": node.get("databaseId"), "name": (node.get("workflow") or {}).get("name"),
            "display_title": node.get("displayTitle"), "status": str(suite.get("status") or "").lower() or None,
            "conclusion": str(suite.get("conclusion") or "").lower() or None, "html_url": node.get("url"),
            "head_sha": (suite.get("commit") or {}).get("oid"), "head_branch": (suite.get("branch") or {}).get("name") or "",
            "event": node.get("event"), "run_attempt": node.get("runAttempt"), "created_at": node.get("createdAt"),
            "updated_at": node.get("updatedAt")}


def upper(value: Any) -> str | None:
    return str(value).upper() if value else None


def slim_pull(doc: dict) -> dict:
    keep = ("number", "html_url", "title", "state", "draft", "merged", "merged_at", "mergeable", "mergeable_state",
            "updated_at")
    head, base = doc.get("head") or {}, doc.get("base") or {}
    return {**{k: doc.get(k) for k in keep}, "head": {"sha": head.get("sha"), "ref": head.get("ref")},
            "base": {"ref": base.get("ref")}}


def slim_check_runs(doc: dict) -> dict:
    keep = ("id", "node_id", "name", "status", "conclusion", "details_url", "started_at", "completed_at")
    return {"total_count": doc.get("total_count"),
            "check_runs": [{**{k: c.get(k) for k in keep}, "suite": (c.get("check_suite") or {}).get("id")}
                           for c in doc.get("check_runs") or [] if isinstance(c, dict)]}


def slim_statuses(doc: dict) -> list[dict]:
    return [{k: c.get(k) for k in ("context", "state", "target_url", "created_at")}
            for c in doc.get("statuses") or [] if isinstance(c, dict)]


def slim_head_runs(doc: dict) -> list[dict]:
    return [{k: r.get(k) for k in ("id", "name", "workflow_id", "check_suite_id", "conclusion")}
            for r in doc.get("workflow_runs") or [] if isinstance(r, dict)]


def slim_workflows(doc: dict) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Workflow id -> name. A run's own REST name is its run-name when the workflow sets one."""
    return {str(w.get("id")): str(w.get("name")) for w in doc.get("workflows") or [] if isinstance(w, dict)}


def slim_rules(doc: Any) -> list[str]:
    """The required checks a branch's rulesets name (GET .../rules/branches/B answers a list)."""
    names = set()
    for rule in doc if isinstance(doc, list) else []:
        if isinstance(rule, dict) and rule.get("type") == "required_status_checks":
            for item in (rule.get("parameters") or {}).get("required_status_checks") or []:
                if isinstance(item, dict) and item.get("context"):
                    names.add(str(item["context"]))
    return sorted(names)


def slim_branch(doc: dict) -> list[str]:
    """The required checks a branch's classic protection names."""
    checks = (doc.get("protection") or {}).get("required_status_checks") or {}
    names = {str(c) for c in checks.get("contexts") or []}
    names |= {str(c["context"]) for c in checks.get("checks") or [] if isinstance(c, dict) and c.get("context")}
    return sorted(names)


def rest_pr_node(pull: dict, checks: dict, statuses: list[dict], runs: list[dict], required: set[str],
                 review: str | None = None, workflows: dict[str, str] | None = None) -> dict:
    """The REST answers in the GraphQL pullRequest shape, so parse_pr reads both alike."""
    suites = {r.get("check_suite_id"): r for r in runs}
    workflows = workflows or {}
    nodes: list[dict] = []
    for c in checks.get("check_runs") or []:
        run = suites.get(c.get("suite"))
        nodes.append({"__typename": "CheckRun", "id": c.get("node_id"), "name": c.get("name"),
                      "status": upper(c.get("status")), "conclusion": upper(c.get("conclusion")),
                      "detailsUrl": c.get("details_url"), "databaseId": c.get("id"), "startedAt": c.get("started_at"),
                      "completedAt": c.get("completed_at"), "isRequired": c.get("name") in required,
                      "checkSuite": {"conclusion": upper(run.get("conclusion")), "workflowRun": {
                          "databaseId": run.get("id"), "workflow": {
                              "name": workflows.get(str(run.get("workflow_id"))) or run.get("name"),
                                                                    "databaseId": run.get("workflow_id")}}}
                      if run else {"conclusion": None, "workflowRun": None}})
    for c in statuses:
        nodes.append({"__typename": "StatusContext", "context": c.get("context"), "state": upper(c.get("state")),
                      "targetUrl": c.get("target_url"), "createdAt": c.get("created_at"),
                      "isRequired": c.get("context") in required})
    rollup = None
    if nodes:
        results = [n.get("conclusion") if n["__typename"] == "CheckRun" else n.get("state") for n in nodes]
        pending = any(n["__typename"] == "CheckRun" and n.get("status") != "COMPLETED" or
                      n["__typename"] == "StatusContext" and n.get("state") in {"PENDING", "EXPECTED"} for n in nodes)
        truncated = (checks.get("total_count") or 0) > len(checks.get("check_runs") or [])
        # GraphQL's rollup state covers every check; ours covers only what was read, so a truncated
        # list is never called green
        state = ("FAILURE" if any(r in FAILED for r in results) else "PENDING" if pending or truncated
                 else "SUCCESS")
        total = (checks.get("total_count") or 0) + len(statuses)
        rollup = {"state": state, "contexts": {"totalCount": max(total, len(nodes)),
                                               "pageInfo": {"hasNextPage": truncated}, "nodes": nodes}}
    head = pull.get("head") or {}
    state = "OPEN" if pull.get("state") == "open" else "MERGED" if pull.get("merged") else "CLOSED"
    mergeable = {True: "MERGEABLE", False: "CONFLICTING"}.get(pull.get("mergeable"), "UNKNOWN")
    return {"number": pull.get("number"), "url": pull.get("html_url"), "title": pull.get("title"), "state": state,
            "isDraft": bool(pull.get("draft")), "merged": bool(pull.get("merged")), "mergedAt": pull.get("merged_at"),
            "mergeable": mergeable, "mergeStateStatus": upper(pull.get("mergeable_state")) or "UNKNOWN",
            "headRefOid": head.get("sha"), "headRefName": head.get("ref"), "reviewDecision": review,
            "updatedAt": pull.get("updated_at"),
            "baseRef": {"branchProtectionRule": {"requiredStatusCheckContexts": sorted(required)}, "rules": {"nodes": []}},
            "commits": {"nodes": [{"commit": {"statusCheckRollup": rollup}}]}}


def parse_comments(node: dict) -> dict:
    comments = node.get("comments") or {}
    return {"url": node.get("url"), "state": node.get("state"), "type": node.get("__typename"),
            "total": comments.get("totalCount"),
            "comments": [{"id": c.get("databaseId"), "url": c.get("url"), "createdAt": c.get("createdAt"),
                          "author": (c.get("author") or {}).get("login"),
                          "body": str(c.get("body") or "")[:COMMENT_BODY_MAX]}
                         for c in comments.get("nodes") or [] if c]}


def brief(check: dict) -> dict:
    return {k: check.get(k) for k in ("name", "workflow", "conclusion", "required", "url")}


def parse_pr(node: dict) -> dict:
    commits = ((node.get("commits") or {}).get("nodes") or [{}])
    rollup = ((commits[0] or {}).get("commit") or {}).get("statusCheckRollup") or {}
    contexts = rollup.get("contexts") or {}
    latest: dict[str, tuple[tuple, dict]] = {}
    superseded: list[dict] = []  # replaced by a newer attempt or run; GitHub's rollup still counts them
    nodes = contexts.get("nodes") or []
    # A run the concurrency group cancelled for a newer run of the same workflow on this commit
    # leaves failed checks (ci-status: cancelled) that the newer run replaces when it gets there.
    def workflow_run(c: dict) -> tuple[object, int]:
        run = (c.get("checkSuite") or {}).get("workflowRun") or {}
        workflow = run.get("workflow") or {}
        return workflow.get("databaseId") or workflow.get("name"), run.get("databaseId") or 0

    newest: dict[object, int] = {}
    for c in nodes:
        workflow, run_id = workflow_run(c)
        if workflow and run_id:
            newest[workflow] = max(newest.get(workflow, 0), run_id)
    for c in nodes:
        workflow_key, run_id = workflow_run(c)
        if c.get("__typename") == "StatusContext":
            state = c.get("state")
            done = state in {"SUCCESS", "FAILURE", "ERROR"}
            check = {"name": c.get("context"), "workflow": None, "status": "COMPLETED" if done else "PENDING",
                     "conclusion": state if done else None, "url": c.get("targetUrl")}
            order = (0, c.get("createdAt") or "")
        else:
            workflow = (((c.get("checkSuite") or {}).get("workflowRun") or {}).get("workflow") or {}).get("name")
            check = {"name": c.get("name"), "workflow": workflow, "status": c.get("status"),
                     "conclusion": c.get("conclusion"), "url": c.get("detailsUrl"), "id": c.get("id"),
                     "runId": run_id or None, "startedAt": c.get("startedAt"), "completedAt": c.get("completedAt")}
            # databaseId first: a queued rerun has no startedAt yet but is still the newer attempt
            order = (c.get("databaseId") or 0, c.get("startedAt") or "")
        check["required"] = bool(c.get("isRequired"))
        if ((c.get("checkSuite") or {}).get("conclusion") == "CANCELLED" and workflow_key and run_id
                and run_id < newest[workflow_key]):
            superseded.append(check)
            continue
        # a rerun leaves the old attempt in the list (cancelled, then passed): keep the newest per
        # (workflow, name), so same-named jobs in different workflows stay separate
        ident = f"{check['workflow'] or ''}\0{check['name']}"
        if ident not in latest or order >= latest[ident][0]:
            if ident in latest:
                superseded.append(latest[ident][1])
            latest[ident] = (order, check)
        else:
            superseded.append(check)
    checks = [check for _, check in latest.values()]
    # only the ones that would have counted against the PR are worth naming
    superseded = [brief(c) for c in superseded if (c.get("conclusion") or "").upper() not in PASSED][:20]
    base = node.get("baseRef") or {}
    required = set((base.get("branchProtectionRule") or {}).get("requiredStatusCheckContexts") or [])
    for rule in ((base.get("rules") or {}).get("nodes") or []):
        for item in ((rule.get("parameters") or {}).get("requiredStatusChecks") or []):
            required.add(item.get("context"))
    # A required check that has not reported yet (ci-status appears only when its run reaches it)
    # is pending, not absent: judging only the required checks present would call a run green early.
    reported = {c.get("name") for c in checks}
    return {
        "requiredMissing": sorted(name for name in required if name and name not in reported),
        "number": node.get("number"), "url": node.get("url"), "title": node.get("title"),
        "state": node.get("state"), "isDraft": node.get("isDraft"), "merged": node.get("merged"),
        "mergedAt": node.get("mergedAt"), "mergeable": node.get("mergeable"),
        "mergeStateStatus": node.get("mergeStateStatus"), "headRefOid": node.get("headRefOid"),
        "headRefName": node.get("headRefName"),
        "reviewDecision": node.get("reviewDecision"), "updatedAt": node.get("updatedAt"),
        "checkState": rollup.get("state"), "checks": checks, "superseded": superseded,
        "checksTotal": contexts.get("totalCount", len(checks)),
        "checksTruncated": bool((contexts.get("pageInfo") or {}).get("hasNextPage")),
    }


def known_steps(pairs: list[tuple[dict, dict]]) -> dict[str, list]:
    """Check node id -> [failedSteps, stepCount] from the cached data: a completed check keeps them."""
    known: dict[str, list] = {}
    for e, _ in pairs:
        for c in (((e["cache"] or {}).get("data") or {}).get("checks") or []):
            if c.get("id") and isinstance(c.get("failedSteps"), list):
                known[c["id"]] = [c["failedSteps"], c.get("stepCount")]
    return known


def slim_run(doc: dict) -> dict:
    keep = ("id", "name", "display_title", "status", "conclusion", "html_url", "head_sha", "head_branch",
            "event", "run_attempt", "created_at", "updated_at")
    return {k: doc.get(k) for k in keep}


def wants_steps(c: dict) -> bool:
    """A check whose steps the daemon reads once: a failure (which steps failed) or a cancellation
    (did any step run: the owned-pool rescue cancels a job still queued for a runner, then re-runs it)."""
    return bool(c.get("id")) and (c.get("conclusion") or "").upper() in STEP_FAILED | {"CANCELLED"}


def failed_steps(steps: list | None) -> list[str]:
    return [str(s.get("name")) for s in steps or [] if isinstance(s, dict)
            and str(s.get("conclusion") or "").upper() in STEP_FAILED]


def slim_jobs(doc: dict) -> list[dict]:
    keep = ("id", "name", "status", "conclusion", "html_url", "run_attempt", "runner_name", "labels",
            "started_at", "completed_at")
    return [{**{k: j.get(k) for k in keep}, "failedSteps": failed_steps(j.get("steps"))}
            for j in doc.get("jobs") or []]


def jobs_attempt(jobs: list[dict] | None) -> int:
    """The newest attempt the job list covers (with filter=latest a re-run of failed jobs keeps the
    jobs that passed at their old attempt)."""
    return max((j.get("run_attempt") or 0 for j in jobs or []), default=0)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- daemon


class Daemon:
    def __init__(self, transport: Callable = http, token: Callable[[], str] = gh_token,
                 controller: Any = "env") -> None:
        self.transport = transport
        # "env" (or any callable) is re-read every tick, so a daemon follows a controller move
        # (cmux-fleet-client's client.json repointed) without a restart; an instance stays fixed.
        self.controller_source: Callable[[], Any] | None = (
            Controller.from_env if controller == "env" else controller if callable(controller) else None)
        self.controller = self.controller_source() if self.controller_source else controller
        self.controller_down_until = 0.0
        self.controller_feed_at = float("-inf")
        self.controller_failures = 0
        self.controller_filled: set[str] = set()  # watched runs the controller tracks, while its feed answers
        self.controller_cursor: int | None = None
        self.controller_seen: dict[str, bool] = {}  # watched run key -> the controller knows the run
        self.controller_status: dict[str, Any] = {"state": "off" if self.controller is None else "starting"}
        self.nudged: set[str] = set()  # PRs a check event touched since their last fetch
        self.token_source = token
        self.token: str | None = None
        doc = read_json(BASE / "budget.json")
        self.budget: dict[str, dict] = doc if isinstance(doc, dict) else {}
        self.saved_budget = json.loads(json.dumps(self.budget))
        self.backoff: dict[str, float] = {"rest": 0.0, "graphql": 0.0}
        self.last_cycle: dict[str, float] = {kind: 0.0 for kind in CYCLES}
        self.now = time.time()
        self.errors: list[str] = []
        self.attempted: dict[str, float] = {}  # when each key was last fetched: a new one at most once per cycle
        self.sent: list[tuple[float, str]] = []  # (time, rest|rest304|graphql) for the last hour's counts
        self.paths: dict[str, str] = dict(BUCKETS)  # the bucket each kind reads over now
        self.reads: list[tuple[float, str, str]] = []  # (time, kind, bucket) per entry read, for the last hour
        self.batch: dict[str, int] = {kind: GQL_BATCH_START for kind in CYCLES}  # entries per GraphQL query
        self.clean: dict[str, int] = {kind: 0 for kind in CYCLES}  # clean GraphQL queries in a row
        # key -> {"at": last lone read, "fails": lone timeouts in a row, "oks": lone answers in a row}
        self.heavy: dict[str, dict] = {}
        self.since: dict[str, float] = {}  # key -> when the daemon first saw it watched, for oldestData
        self.beat = float("-inf")
        self.status: dict[str, Any] = {}  # the last good tick's daemon.json
        self.failing: dict[str, Any] | None = None  # set while ticks raise: the latest error and since when
        self.tick_deadline = float("inf")  # time.monotonic() after which this tick starts no request
        self.deferred: set[str] = set()  # keys a tick ran out of time for: read first on the next tick

    # -- budget

    def mode(self, bucket: str) -> str:
        """normal, lean (below the floor) or stop (below the reserve, or told to back off)."""
        if self.backoff[bucket] > self.now:
            return "stop"
        seen = self.budget.get(bucket) or {}
        if not seen or (seen.get("reset") or 0) <= self.now:
            return "normal"
        floor, reserve = FLOORS[bucket]
        remaining = seen.get("remaining", floor)
        return "stop" if remaining < reserve else "lean" if remaining < floor else "normal"

    def richness(self, bucket: str) -> float:
        """What this bucket can still spend per second until its own reset, above its reserve; -1
        while it is stopped. A bucket not seen yet, or past its reset, counts as a full hour's quota."""
        if self.mode(bucket) == "stop":
            return -1.0
        seen = self.budget.get(bucket) or {}
        limit = seen.get("limit") or 5000
        reserve = FLOORS[bucket][1]
        if not seen or (seen.get("reset") or 0) <= self.now:
            return max(0, limit - reserve) / FULL_WINDOW
        return max(0, seen.get("remaining", limit) - reserve) / max(seen["reset"] - self.now, RICH_WINDOW_MIN)

    def route(self, kind: str) -> str:
        """The bucket this kind reads over this tick: the richer one. A kind leaves its current bucket
        only when the other is SWITCH_MARGIN richer (or the current one stopped), so the reads move in
        steps instead of flapping, and the two buckets drain about evenly."""
        current = self.paths[kind]
        if kind in BALANCED:
            here, there = self.richness(current), self.richness(OTHER[current])
            if there >= 0 and (here < 0 or there > here * SWITCH_MARGIN):
                log({"event": "route", "kind": kind, "from": current, "to": OTHER[current],
                     "perMinute": {current: round(here * 60, 1), OTHER[current]: round(there * 60, 1)}})
                self.paths[kind] = current = OTHER[current]
        return current

    def note_budget(self, bucket: str, limit: Any, remaining: Any, reset: float | None) -> None:
        try:
            self.budget[bucket] = {"limit": int(limit), "remaining": int(remaining), "reset": reset, "seenAt": self.now}
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            pass

    # -- requests

    def request(self, method: str, path: str, body: dict | None = None, etag: str | None = None) -> tuple[int, dict, Any]:
        if time.monotonic() >= self.tick_deadline:
            raise OutOfTime
        if self.token is None:
            self.token = self.token_source()
        headers = {"Authorization": f"Bearer {self.token}", "Accept": "application/vnd.github+json",
                   "User-Agent": "glaeda-gh", "X-GitHub-Api-Version": "2022-11-28"}
        if etag:
            headers["If-None-Match"] = etag
        raw_body = json.dumps(body).encode() if body is not None else None
        if raw_body is not None:
            headers["Content-Type"] = "application/json"
        status, h, raw = self.transport(method, API + path, headers, raw_body)
        bucket = "graphql" if path == "/graphql" else "rest"
        self.sent.append((self.now, "rest304" if bucket == "rest" and status == 304 else bucket))
        if status == 401:
            self.token = None  # re-read from gh next time
        if "x-ratelimit-remaining" in h and h.get("x-ratelimit-resource", "core") in {"core", "graphql"}:
            try:
                reset = float(h.get("x-ratelimit-reset", ""))
            except ValueError:
                reset = None
            self.note_budget(bucket, h.get("x-ratelimit-limit"), h.get("x-ratelimit-remaining"), reset)
        if status in {403, 429} and (h.get("retry-after") or h.get("x-ratelimit-remaining") == "0"):
            try:
                wait = float(h["retry-after"]) if h.get("retry-after") else float(h.get("x-ratelimit-reset", 0)) - self.now
            except ValueError:
                wait = CYCLE
            self.backoff[bucket] = self.now + max(CYCLE, wait)
        try:
            doc = json.loads(raw) if raw else None
        except ValueError:
            doc = None
        return status, h, doc

    # -- one tick

    def watches(self) -> list[dict]:
        """Live watch entries; expired ones are removed here."""
        out = []
        for path in sorted(watch_dir().glob("*.json")) if watch_dir().is_dir() else []:
            doc = read_json(path)
            try:
                age = self.now - path.stat().st_mtime
            except OSError:
                continue
            if not isinstance(doc, dict) or "key" not in doc:
                path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                continue
            key = doc["key"]
            try:
                parse_key(split_key(key)[0], key.split(":", 1)[1])
            except (WatchError, ValueError, IndexError):
                path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                continue
            cached = read_cache(key)
            kind = split_key(key)[0]
            # a comment watch only serves a wait that is running: it touches the file every 30 s
            done = kind == "comment" or (cached is not None and terminal(kind, cached.get("data")))
            if age > IDLE_EXPIRY or (done and age > TERMINAL_GRACE):
                path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                continue
            out.append({"key": key, "jobs": bool(doc.get("jobs")), "cache": cached, "touched": self.now - age})
        return out

    def due(self, kind: str, entries: list[dict]) -> list[dict]:
        mine = [e for e in entries if e["key"].startswith(kind + ":")]
        mode = self.mode(self.route(kind))
        if mode == "stop":
            return []
        interval = CYCLES[kind] * (LEAN_FACTOR if mode == "lean" else 1)
        full = self.now - self.last_cycle[kind] >= interval
        if full:
            self.last_cycle[kind] = self.now
        out = [e for e in mine if e["key"] in self.deferred]  # the last tick ran out of time for these
        first = {e["key"] for e in out}
        self.deferred -= first
        for e in mine:
            c = e["cache"]
            if e["key"] in first:
                continue
            if (kind == "pr" and c is not None and e["key"] in self.nudged
                    and self.now - self.attempted.get(e["key"], float("-inf")) >= NUDGE_MIN):
                self.nudged.discard(e["key"])  # a check event on its head: re-read now, not at the cycle
                out.append(e)
                continue
            if kind == "run" and c is not None and e["key"] in self.controller_filled:
                data = c.get("data") or {}
                # A failed attempt whose job list is not this attempt's: REST reads it once (per cycle
                # at most), for the failed steps and to tell a runner's refusal from a real failure.
                failed = (data.get("status") == "completed" and data.get("conclusion") not in RUN_OK
                          and jobs_attempt(c.get("jobs")) < (data.get("run_attempt") or 1)
                          and self.now - self.attempted.get(e["key"], float("-inf")) >= CYCLES[kind])
                # A finished run with a reader newer than both its data and the last REST read: that
                # reader's wait will not rule on older data, so REST confirms it once (it may have
                # been rerun before the webhook said so).
                confirm = terminal(kind, data) and e["touched"] > max(c.get("dataAt") or 0, c.get("restAt") or 0)
                # Otherwise the controller answered on this tick: REST only as the safety net.
                if failed or confirm or self.now - (c.get("restAt") or 0) >= REST_SAFETY:
                    out.append(e)
                continue
            if c is None:
                # a new entry is filled at once, but at most once per cycle when the fetch never gets as
                # far as storing an answer (a failed fetch stores its error, and then waits for full cycles)
                if self.now - self.attempted.get(e["key"], float("-inf")) >= CYCLES[kind]:
                    out.append(e)
            elif full and (not terminal(kind, c.get("data")) or self.now - c.get("fetchedAt", 0) >= TERMINAL_REFRESH
                           # a reader came after the last fetch: a rerun or reopen may have changed it
                           or e["touched"] > c.get("fetchedAt", 0)):
                out.append(e)
        for e in out:
            self.attempted[e["key"]] = self.now
        return out

    def defer(self, entries: list[dict]) -> None:
        self.deferred.update(e["key"] for e in entries)

    def tick(self, now: float | None = None) -> dict:
        self.now = time.time() if now is None else now
        self.tick_deadline = time.monotonic() + TICK_BUDGET
        self.errors = []
        entries = self.watches()
        try:
            self.from_controller(entries)
        except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001  the controller is an optional, free source: a fault
            # there must not stop GitHub polling (it did: glaeda#1305). A WatchError is expected and
            # says what failed; anything else is a bug, so it carries its code site.
            self.controller_down(error, None if isinstance(error, WatchError) else error_site(error))
        prs, runs, comments = self.due("pr", entries), self.due("run", entries), self.due("comment", entries)
        over = {kind: self.paths[kind] for kind in BALANCED}  # due() routed each kind
        gql_prs, gql_runs = (prs if over["pr"] == "graphql" else []), (runs if over["run"] == "graphql" else [])
        for kind, batch, fetch in (("pr", gql_prs, self.fetch_prs), ("comment", comments, self.fetch_comments),
                                   ("run", gql_runs, self.fetch_runs_graphql)):
            self.batched(kind, batch, fetch)
        over_rest = (prs if over["pr"] == "rest" else []) + (runs if over["run"] == "rest" else [])
        for i, e in enumerate(over_rest):
            if self.mode("rest") == "stop":
                break
            try:
                (self.fetch_pr_rest if e["key"].startswith("pr:") else self.fetch_run)(e)
            except OutOfTime:
                self.defer(over_rest[i:])
                break
            except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001
                self.errors.append(f"{e['key']}: {describe(error)}")
        self.tick_deadline = float("inf")
        self.deferred &= {e["key"] for e in entries}
        self.attempted = {k: t for k, t in self.attempted.items() if self.now - t < 2 * IDLE_EXPIRY}
        oldest = self.oldest_data(entries, {e["key"] for e in prs + runs + comments})
        self.prune({e["key"] for e in entries})
        if self.budget != self.saved_budget:
            write_json(BASE / "budget.json", self.budget)
            self.saved_budget = json.loads(json.dumps(self.budget))
        self.sent = [(t, what) for t, what in self.sent if self.now - t < 3600]
        hour = {what: sum(1 for _, w in self.sent if w == what) for what in ("rest", "rest304", "graphql")}
        self.reads = [r for r in self.reads if self.now - r[0] < 3600]
        reads = {kind: {b: sum(1 for _, k, w in self.reads if k == kind and w == b) for b in ("graphql", "rest")}
                 for kind in CYCLES}
        status = {"pid": os.getpid(), "at": self.now, "watching": len(entries),
                  "fetched": {"pr": len(prs), "run": len(runs), "comment": len(comments)},
                  "controller": {**self.controller_status, "runs": len(self.controller_filled)},
                  "modes": {b: self.mode(b) for b in ("rest", "graphql")}, "errors": self.errors[:10],
                  "lastHour": hour, "heartbeat": HEARTBEAT_EVERY, "paths": dict(self.paths), "reads": reads,
                  "perMinute": {b: round(self.richness(b) * 60, 1) for b in ("rest", "graphql")},
                  "batch": dict(self.batch), "oldestData": oldest, "deferred": len(self.deferred)}
        # the heartbeat lets clients tell a hung daemon (lock held, nothing written) from an idle one
        recovered, self.failing = self.failing is not None, None
        if recovered or prs or runs or comments or self.errors or self.now - self.beat >= HEARTBEAT_EVERY:
            write_json(BASE / "daemon.json", status)
            self.beat = self.now
        self.status = status
        return status

    def batched(self, kind: str, entries: list[dict], fetch: Callable[[list[dict]], str | None]) -> None:
        """Read `entries` over GraphQL in queries of self.batch[kind]. A query that times out (504)
        halves the batch (once per tick, never below GQL_BATCH_MIN) and is retried at once as two
        halves, so one heavy entry cannot keep the rest stale: it ends up alone, is remembered as
        heavy and read alone from then on (every LEAN_FACTOR cycles once it keeps timing out), and
        the batch grows back. A 502 or 503 is split and retried without shrinking. GQL_SPLITS bounds
        the extra queries a tick spends on splitting; what is left waits for the next cycle."""
        self.heavy = {k: h for k, h in self.heavy.items() if self.now - h["at"] < IDLE_EXPIRY}
        size = self.batch[kind]
        alone, rest = [], []
        for e in entries:
            h = self.heavy.get(e["key"])
            if h is None:
                rest.append(e)
            elif h["fails"] < HEAVY_AFTER or self.now - h["at"] >= LEAN_FACTOR * CYCLES[kind]:
                alone.append([e])
        queue = alone + [rest[i:i + size] for i in range(0, len(rest), size)]
        splits, shrunk = 0, False
        while queue:
            chunk = queue.pop(0)
            if self.mode("graphql") == "stop":
                return
            try:
                error = fetch(chunk)
            except OutOfTime:
                self.defer(chunk + [e for c in queue for e in c])
                return
            except Exception as failure:  # noqa: BLE001  one bad batch must not stop the rest
                self.errors.append(f"graphql: {describe(failure)}")
                continue
            if len(chunk) == 1 and chunk[0]["key"] in self.heavy or (len(chunk) == 1 and error and SLOW.search(error)):
                # a lone read says nothing about the batch size; it only judges this entry
                h = self.heavy.setdefault(chunk[0]["key"], {"at": self.now, "fails": 0, "oks": 0})
                h["at"] = self.now
                if error is None:
                    h["fails"], h["oks"] = 0, h["oks"] + 1
                    if h["oks"] >= 2:
                        self.heavy.pop(chunk[0]["key"])  # answered twice alone: back into the batches
                elif SLOW.search(error):
                    h["fails"], h["oks"] = h["fails"] + 1, 0
                continue
            if error is None:
                self.clean[kind] += 1
                if self.clean[kind] >= GQL_GROW_AFTER and len(chunk) >= self.batch[kind] < GQL_BATCH:
                    self.batch[kind] += 1
                    self.clean[kind] = 0
                continue
            slow = bool(SLOW.search(error))
            if not slow and not FLAKY.search(error) or len(chunk) < 2:
                continue
            self.clean[kind] = 0
            if slow and not shrunk and self.batch[kind] > GQL_BATCH_MIN:
                shrunk = True
                self.batch[kind] = max(GQL_BATCH_MIN, self.batch[kind] // 2)
                log({"event": "batch", "kind": kind, "size": self.batch[kind], "after": error})
            if splits + 2 <= GQL_SPLITS:
                splits += 2
                half = (len(chunk) + 1) // 2
                left = [e for c in queue for e in c]
                size = self.batch[kind]
                queue = [chunk[:half], chunk[half:]] + [left[i:i + size] for i in range(0, len(left), size)]

    def oldest_data(self, entries: list[dict], fetched: set[str]) -> dict[str, float | None]:
        """Per kind, how old the oldest data of an open (not finished) watch is. A finished one is
        re-read only every TERMINAL_REFRESH by design, so it does not count."""
        oldest: dict[str, float | None] = {kind: None for kind in CYCLES}
        self.since = {e["key"]: self.since.get(e["key"], self.now) for e in entries}
        for e in entries:
            kind = split_key(e["key"])[0]
            cached = read_cache(e["key"]) if e["key"] in fetched else e["cache"]
            if cached and terminal(kind, cached.get("data")):
                continue
            # never read yet (a heavy PR timing out since it was registered): as old as its watch here
            seen = (cached or {}).get("dataAt") or self.since[e["key"]]
            age_s = max(0.0, self.now - seen)
            oldest[kind] = max(oldest[kind] or 0.0, age_s)
        return oldest

    def tick_failed(self, error: str, site: str, now: float | None = None) -> None:
        """A tick raised. The daemon is alive but refreshes nothing, so keep the heartbeat and say
        why: without this, a tick that raised every time looked like a hung daemon to clients, and a
        restart (which could not help) was their only advice."""
        self.now = time.time() if now is None else now
        first = self.failing is None
        since = self.now if first else self.failing["since"]
        count = 1 if first else self.failing["count"] + 1
        self.failing = {"error": error, "site": site, "since": since, "count": count}
        if first or self.now - self.beat >= HEARTBEAT_EVERY:
            write_json(BASE / "daemon.json", {**self.status, "pid": os.getpid(), "at": self.now,
                                              "heartbeat": HEARTBEAT_EVERY, "failing": self.failing})
            self.beat = self.now

    def store(self, key: str, prior: dict | None, **fields: Any) -> None:
        entry = {"key": key, "fetchedAt": self.now, "dataAt": None, "data": None, "error": None}
        if prior:
            entry.update({k: prior[k] for k in ("dataAt", "data", "etag", "jobs", "jobsEtag", "jobsAt", "restAt", "source", "restParts")
                          if k in prior})
        entry.update(fields)
        write_json(cache_dir() / file_name(key), entry)

    def fail(self, entries: list[dict], message: str) -> None:
        self.errors.append(message)
        for e in entries:
            self.store(e["key"], e["cache"], error=message)

    def graphql(self, query: str, variables: dict) -> tuple[dict | None, str | None]:
        """(doc, None) or (None, error message). Notes the rateLimit field when the query asks for it."""
        try:
            status, _, doc = self.request("POST", "/graphql", {"query": query, "variables": variables})
        except (WatchError, OSError) as error:
            return None, f"graphql: {describe(error)}"
        if status != 200 or not isinstance(doc, dict):
            return None, f"graphql: HTTP {status}"
        rate = (doc.get("data") or {}).get("rateLimit") or {}
        if rate:
            self.note_budget("graphql", rate.get("limit"), rate.get("remaining"), iso_epoch(rate.get("resetAt")))
        return doc, None

    def fetch_aliased(self, entries: list[dict], kind: str) -> str | None:
        """None, or the error when the whole query failed (batched() halves on a timeout)."""
        query, variables = pr_query([e["key"] for e in entries], kind)
        self.reads += [(self.now, kind, "graphql")] * len(entries)
        doc, error = self.graphql(query, variables)
        if doc is None:
            self.fail(entries, error or "graphql: no response")
            return error or "graphql: no response"
        data = doc.get("data") or {}
        if not data and doc.get("errors"):  # GitHub reports a timeout as 200 with data null
            message = "graphql: " + str((doc["errors"][0] or {}).get("message"))[:200]
            self.fail(entries, message)
            return message
        problems: dict[str, dict] = {}
        for err in doc.get("errors") or []:
            path = err.get("path") or [""]
            problems[str(path[0])] = err
        alias, field = ("p", "pullRequest") if kind == "pr" else ("c", "issueOrPullRequest")
        parsed = {}
        for i, e in enumerate(entries):
            node = (data.get(f"{alias}{i}") or {}).get(field)
            if node:
                parsed[i] = parse_pr(node) if kind == "pr" else parse_comments(node)
        if kind == "pr":
            self.fill_steps([(entries[i], d) for i, d in parsed.items()])
        for i, e in enumerate(entries):
            if i in parsed:
                # a REST read's kept answers are dead weight in a file clients re-read every 3 s
                extra = {"restParts": None} if kind == "pr" else {}
                self.store(e["key"], e["cache"], dataAt=self.now, data=parsed[i], **extra)
                continue
            err = problems.get(f"{alias}{i}") or {}
            what = "pull request" if kind == "pr" else "issue or pull request"
            # only GitHub saying NOT_FOUND for this alias means it does not exist (or is not visible)
            self.store(e["key"], e["cache"], error=str(err.get("message") or f"no data for this {what}")[:200],
                       missing=err.get("type") == "NOT_FOUND")

    def fetch_prs(self, entries: list[dict]) -> str | None:
        return self.fetch_aliased(entries, "pr")

    def fetch_comments(self, entries: list[dict]) -> str | None:
        return self.fetch_aliased(entries, "comment")

    def fill_steps(self, pairs: list[tuple[dict, dict]]) -> None:
        """Name the failed steps of each failed check run. A completed check does not change, so a
        check whose steps an earlier fetch read keeps them and costs nothing; the rest share one
        small query. When that query fails the checks go without, and the next cycle tries again."""
        known = known_steps(pairs)
        want: dict[str, list[dict]] = {}
        owners: dict[str, set[str]] = {}  # check id -> the keys of the PRs that show it
        for e, data in pairs:
            for c in data.get("checks") or []:
                if not wants_steps(c):
                    continue
                if c["id"] in known:
                    c["failedSteps"], c["stepCount"] = known[c["id"]]
                else:
                    want.setdefault(c["id"], []).append(c)
                    owners.setdefault(c["id"], set()).add(e["key"])
        ids = list(want)[:2 * STEPS_BATCH]  # the rest wait a cycle (failed and cancelled checks; each is read once)
        for i in range(0, len(ids), STEPS_BATCH):
            if self.mode("graphql") == "stop":
                return
            try:
                doc, error = self.graphql(STEPS_QUERY, {"ids": ids[i:i + STEPS_BATCH]})
            except OutOfTime:  # the checks go without their steps; their PRs go first next tick
                for check_id in ids[i:]:
                    self.deferred.update(owners[check_id])
                return
            if doc is None:
                self.errors.append(f"steps: {error}")
                return
            for node in (doc.get("data") or {}).get("nodes") or []:
                if isinstance(node, dict) and node.get("id") in want:
                    steps = (node.get("steps") or {}).get("nodes")
                    names = failed_steps(steps)
                    for c in want[node["id"]]:
                        # steps null (a partial error) is unknown, never "no step ran"
                        c["failedSteps"], c["stepCount"] = names, len(steps) if isinstance(steps, list) else None

    def get(self, path: str, etag: str | None) -> tuple[str, Any, str | None]:
        """("fresh", doc, etag), ("same", None, etag) on 304, or ("error", message, None)."""
        try:
            status, h, doc = self.request("GET", path, etag=etag)
        except (WatchError, OSError) as error:
            return "error", f"rest: {describe(error)}", None
        if status == 304:
            return "same", None, etag
        if status == 200 and isinstance(doc, (dict, list)):  # .../rules/branches/B answers a list
            return "fresh", doc, h.get("etag")
        message = (doc or {}).get("message", "") if isinstance(doc, dict) else ""
        return "error", f"HTTP {status} {message}".strip()[:200], None

    def fetch_run(self, e: dict) -> None:
        _, owner, repo, run_id = split_key(e["key"])
        prior = e["cache"] or {}
        self.reads.append((self.now, "run", "rest"))
        base = f"/repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs/{run_id}"
        state, doc, etag = self.get(base, prior.get("etag") if prior.get("data") else None)
        if state == "error":
            self.errors.append(f"{e['key']}: {doc}")
            return self.store(e["key"], e["cache"], error=doc)
        # restAt: the last direct read from GitHub, over either bucket (the controller's safety-net clock)
        fields: dict[str, Any] = {"error": None, "etag": etag, "dataAt": self.now, "restAt": self.now}
        if state == "fresh":
            fields["data"] = slim_run(doc)
            # a run the controller tracks keeps that source; REST was only the safety-net check
            if prior.get("source") != "controller" or \
                    (iso_epoch(doc.get("updated_at")) or 0) > (iso_epoch((prior.get("data") or {}).get("updated_at")) or 0):
                fields["source"] = "rest"
        self.read_jobs(e, fields, state == "fresh")

    def fetch_runs_graphql(self, entries: list[dict]) -> str | None:
        """Runs over GraphQL, for when REST is the poorer bucket: one aliased query by run URL. A run
        GraphQL cannot resolve is an error, as REST's 404 is, not a missing entry."""
        query, variables = run_query([e["key"] for e in entries])
        self.reads += [(self.now, "run", "graphql")] * len(entries)
        doc, error = self.graphql(query, variables)
        if doc is None:
            self.fail(entries, error or "graphql: no response")
            return error or "graphql: no response"
        data = doc.get("data") or {}
        problems = {str((err.get("path") or [""])[0]): err for err in doc.get("errors") or [] if isinstance(err, dict)}
        if not data and problems:  # the whole query failed (a schema change, say): say what GitHub said
            message = "graphql: " + str(next(iter(problems.values())).get("message"))[:200]
            self.fail(entries, message)
            return message
        for i, e in enumerate(entries):
            node = data.get(f"w{i}")
            if not isinstance(node, dict) or node.get("__typename") != "WorkflowRun":
                message = "graphql: " + str((problems.get(f"w{i}") or {}).get("message") or "no workflow run at this URL")[:200]
                self.errors.append(f"{e['key']}: {message}")
                self.store(e["key"], e["cache"], error=message)
                continue
            prior = e["cache"] or {}
            before = prior.get("data") or {}
            run = graphql_run(node)
            run["head_branch"] = run["head_branch"] or before.get("head_branch") or ""
            fresh = any(run.get(k) != before.get(k) for k in ("status", "conclusion", "run_attempt", "updated_at"))
            fields: dict[str, Any] = {"error": None, "data": run, "dataAt": self.now, "restAt": self.now}
            if prior.get("source") != "controller" or \
                    (iso_epoch(run.get("updated_at")) or 0) > (iso_epoch(before.get("updated_at")) or 0):
                fields["source"] = "graphql"
            self.read_jobs(e, fields, fresh)

    def read_jobs(self, e: dict, fields: dict[str, Any], fresh: bool) -> None:
        """Store a run read, with its job list when wanted. Job lists come from REST on either path:
        only REST names the runner and its labels, which tell a runner's refusal from a failure."""
        _, owner, repo, run_id = split_key(e["key"])
        prior = e["cache"] or {}
        base = f"/repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs/{run_id}"
        run = fields.get("data") or prior.get("data") or {}
        # A failed attempt's job list names the failed steps, and tells a runner's refusal from a real
        # failure. It is read once per attempt, lean or not: callers would otherwise each read it.
        failed_attempt = (run.get("status") == "completed" and run.get("conclusion") not in RUN_OK
                          and self.mode("rest") != "stop"
                          and (fresh or jobs_attempt(prior.get("jobs")) < (run.get("run_attempt") or 1)))
        if (e["jobs"] and self.mode("rest") == "normal") or failed_attempt:
            try:
                jstate, jdoc, jetag = self.get(base + "/jobs?per_page=100", prior.get("jobsEtag") if "jobs" in prior else None)
            except OutOfTime:  # the run itself is read: store it, and its jobs wait for a later read
                jstate, jdoc, jetag = "late", None, None
            if jstate == "fresh":
                fields.update(jobs=slim_jobs(jdoc), jobsEtag=jetag, jobsAt=self.now)
            elif jstate == "same":
                fields["jobsAt"] = self.now
            elif jstate == "late":
                self.deferred.add(e["key"])
            else:
                self.errors.append(f"{e['key']} jobs: {jdoc}")
        self.store(e["key"], e["cache"], **fields)

    def rest_part(self, old: dict, new: dict, path: str, slim: Callable[[Any], Any], absent: Any = None) -> Any:
        """A conditional GET whose slimmed answer is kept with its ETag: an unchanged resource answers
        304, which costs nothing, and its kept answer is reused. Raises WatchError on an error, except
        a 404 when `absent` is given: then the last answer stands, or `absent` without one."""
        kept = old.get(path) or {}
        state, doc, etag = self.get(path, kept.get("etag") if "doc" in kept else None)
        if state == "error" and absent is not None and str(doc).startswith("HTTP 404"):
            new[path] = {"doc": kept["doc"]} if "doc" in kept else {"doc": absent}
            return new[path]["doc"]
        if state == "error":
            raise WatchError(doc)
        new[path] = kept if state == "same" else {"etag": etag, "doc": slim(doc)}
        return new[path]["doc"]

    def fetch_pr_rest(self, e: dict) -> None:
        """One PR over REST, for when GraphQL is the poorer bucket: the pull, its head's check runs,
        statuses and workflow runs (for superseded runs), the repo's workflow names, and the base
        branch's required checks from its rulesets and classic protection. Every read is conditional,
        so a quiet PR costs nothing. A failed read keeps the cached data and says why."""
        _, owner, repo, number = split_key(e["key"])
        prior = e["cache"] or {}
        self.reads.append((self.now, "pr", "rest"))
        root = f"/repos/{owner}/{repo}"
        old, parts = prior.get("restParts") or {}, {}
        try:
            pull = self.rest_part(old, parts, f"{root}/pulls/{number}", slim_pull)
            sha = (pull.get("head") or {}).get("sha") or ""
            base = urllib.parse.quote(str((pull.get("base") or {}).get("ref") or ""), safe="/")
            # filter=all: every attempt, as GraphQL's rollup lists them, so parse_pr dedupes both alike
            path = f"{root}/commits/{sha}/check-runs?filter=all&per_page=100"
            checks = self.rest_part(old, parts, path, slim_check_runs)
            listed = list(checks.get("check_runs") or [])
            for page in range(2, min(REST_CHECK_PAGES, -(-(checks.get("total_count") or 0) // 100)) + 1):
                listed += self.rest_part(old, parts, f"{path}&page={page}", slim_check_runs).get("check_runs") or []
            checks = {"total_count": checks.get("total_count"), "check_runs": listed}
            statuses = self.rest_part(old, parts, f"{root}/commits/{sha}/status?per_page=100", slim_statuses)
            runs = self.rest_part(old, parts, f"{root}/actions/runs?head_sha={sha}&per_page=100", slim_head_runs)
            workflows = self.rest_part(old, parts, f"{root}/actions/workflows?per_page=100", slim_workflows)
            # a deleted base (a stacked PR's, after its merge) has no requirements left to read
            required = set(self.rest_part(old, parts, f"{root}/rules/branches/{base}?per_page=100", slim_rules, []))
            required |= set(self.rest_part(old, parts, f"{root}/branches/{base}", slim_branch, []))
        except WatchError as error:
            message = f"rest: {error}"
            self.errors.append(f"{e['key']}: {message}")
            # only GitHub answering 404 for the pull itself means it does not exist (or is not visible)
            return self.store(e["key"], e["cache"], error=message[:200],
                              missing=not parts and str(error).startswith("HTTP 404"))
        review = (prior.get("data") or {}).get("reviewDecision")  # REST has none: keep GraphQL's last
        data = parse_pr(rest_pr_node(pull, checks, statuses, runs, required, review, workflows))
        self.fill_steps_rest(e, data, root, {c.get("node_id"): c.get("id") for c in listed})
        self.store(e["key"], e["cache"], dataAt=self.now, data=data, restParts=parts)

    def fill_steps_rest(self, e: dict, data: dict, root: str, job_ids: dict[str, Any]) -> None:
        """fill_steps over REST, for a PR read over REST (GraphQL is the poorer bucket, or stopped).
        An Actions check run's id is its job's id, and GET .../actions/jobs/ID lists the steps. A
        completed job does not change, so each is read once; at most REST_STEPS_MAX per read, and
        the rest on the next one. Without its steps a refused or never-started check cannot be held."""
        known = known_steps([(e, data)])
        left = REST_STEPS_MAX
        for c in data.get("checks") or []:
            if not wants_steps(c):
                continue
            if c["id"] in known:
                c["failedSteps"], c["stepCount"] = known[c["id"]]
                continue
            job_id = job_ids.get(c["id"])
            if not job_id or not c.get("runId") or left <= 0 or self.mode("rest") == "stop":
                continue
            left -= 1
            try:
                state, doc, _ = self.get(f"{root}/actions/jobs/{job_id}", None)
            except OutOfTime:  # the PR's checks and statuses are read; it goes first next tick for the steps
                self.deferred.add(e["key"])
                return
            if state != "fresh" or not isinstance(doc, dict):
                self.errors.append(f"{e['key']} steps: {doc}")
                continue
            steps = doc.get("steps")
            c["failedSteps"], c["stepCount"] = failed_steps(steps), len(steps) if isinstance(steps, list) else None

    def controller_down(self, error: Exception, site: str | None = None) -> None:
        """Leave the controller alone for a while; REST serves meanwhile. The cursor is kept, so the
        feed catches up on what happened in between when it answers again."""
        what = describe(error) + (f" at {site}" if site else "")
        if isinstance(error, NoReceiver):
            wait, state = CONTROLLER_ABSENT, "absent"  # not an error: that controller has no receiver
        else:
            self.controller_failures += 1
            wait, state = min(CONTROLLER_ABSENT, CONTROLLER_RETRY * 2 ** (self.controller_failures - 1)), "down"
            self.errors.append(what)
        self.controller_down_until = self.now + wait
        self.controller_status = {"state": state, "error": what, "retryAt": self.now + wait,
                                  "token": bool(getattr(self.controller, "token", None))}

    def read_feed(self) -> list[dict]:
        """The feed pages after the cursor. The first call only learns the cursor; a reset (the
        controller lost its state) starts over, so every watched run gets a fresh baseline."""
        if self.controller_cursor is None:
            doc = self.controller.events(None)
            self.controller_cursor, self.controller_seen = doc["cursor"], {}
            return [doc]
        pages = []
        for _ in range(FEED_PAGES):
            doc = self.controller.events(self.controller_cursor)
            if doc.get("reset"):
                self.controller_cursor, self.controller_seen = doc["cursor"], {}
                return [doc]
            pages.append(doc)
            self.controller_cursor = doc["cursor"]
            if not doc.get("more"):
                break
        return pages

    def follow_controller(self) -> None:
        """Switch to the controller the source names now. Its feed starts over: a cursor is only
        meaningful to the controller that issued it, and the old one, drained, serves stale reads."""
        if self.controller_source is None:
            return
        if (self.controller_source is Controller.from_env and not os.environ.get("CMUX_CI_CONTROLLER")
                and CONTROLLER_CONFIG.exists() and not isinstance(read_json(CONTROLLER_CONFIG), dict)):
            return  # a config caught mid-write reads as garbage: keep the controller rather than the default
        current = self.controller_source()
        if getattr(current, "base", None) == getattr(self.controller, "base", None):
            if current is not None and self.controller is not None:
                self.controller.token = current.token  # a rotated token applies without a restart
            return
        log({"event": "controller", "from": getattr(self.controller, "base", None),
             "to": getattr(current, "base", None)})
        self.controller = current
        self.controller_cursor, self.controller_seen = None, {}
        self.controller_down_until, self.controller_failures = 0.0, 0
        self.controller_status = {"state": "off" if current is None else "starting"}

    def from_controller(self, entries: list[dict]) -> None:
        """Follow the controller's change feed: free, one request every CONTROLLER_FEED_EVERY seconds,
        plus one baseline read for each run newly watched. Job lists still come from REST."""
        self.controller_filled = set()
        self.follow_controller()
        if self.controller is None or self.now < self.controller_down_until:
            return
        deadline = time.monotonic() + TICK  # a slow controller must not stall the tick
        pages: list[dict] = []
        if self.now - self.controller_feed_at >= CONTROLLER_FEED_EVERY:
            self.controller_feed_at = self.now
            try:
                pages = self.read_feed()
            except WatchError as error:
                return self.controller_down(error)
            self.controller_failures = 0
        runs = {e["key"]: e for e in entries if e["key"].startswith("run:") and not e["jobs"]}
        self.controller_seen = {k: v for k, v in self.controller_seen.items() if k in runs}
        for key, e in runs.items():
            if key in self.controller_seen:
                continue
            if time.monotonic() > deadline:
                break  # the rest get their baseline on the next tick; REST covers them meanwhile
            _, owner, repo, run_id = split_key(key)
            try:
                run = self.controller.run(run_id)
            except WatchError as error:
                return self.controller_down(error)
            known = run is not None and (run.get("repo") or "").lower() == f"{owner}/{repo}"
            self.controller_seen[key] = known
            if known:
                self.apply_run(e, run)
        for page in pages:
            for run in page.get("runs") or []:
                key = f"run:{str(run.get('repo') or '').lower()}/{run.get('id')}"
                if key in runs:
                    self.controller_seen[key] = True  # newer than any baseline
                    self.apply_run(runs[key], run)
            self.nudge(entries, page)
            self.merge_comments(entries, page)
        self.controller_filled = {k for k, known in self.controller_seen.items() if known}
        if pages:
            self.controller_status = {"state": "ok", "lastDelivery": pages[-1].get("receiver_last_delivery"),
                                      "token": bool(self.controller.token)}

    def apply_run(self, e: dict, run: dict) -> None:
        prior = e["cache"] or {}
        before = prior.get("data") or {}
        older = iso_epoch(run.get("updated_at")) or 0
        if prior.get("source") in {"rest", "graphql"} and (iso_epoch(before.get("updated_at")) or 0) > older:
            return  # a lost delivery: GitHub has shown a newer state than the controller
        data = controller_run(run)
        for k in ("display_title", "created_at"):  # the webhook record lacks these; keep REST's
            data[k] = before.get(k)
        same = prior.get("source") == "controller" and all(
            before.get(k) == data.get(k) for k in ("status", "conclusion", "run_attempt", "updated_at"))
        fields: dict[str, Any] = {"error": None, "data": data, "source": "controller"}
        if not same:
            fields["dataAt"] = self.now  # unchanged data keeps its time, so wait cannot rule on it anew
        if prior.get("source") != "controller":
            fields["restAt"] = self.now  # the safety-net clock starts when the controller takes over
        self.store(e["key"], e["cache"], **fields)
        e["cache"] = read_cache(e["key"])

    def nudge(self, entries: list[dict], page: dict) -> None:
        """Mark watched PRs whose head commit or branch had a run or check suite event."""
        touched: set[tuple[str, str]] = set()
        for item in (page.get("runs") or []) + (page.get("suites") or []):
            repo = str(item.get("repo") or "").lower()
            touched.add((repo, "sha:" + str(item.get("head_sha") or "").lower()))
            if item.get("head_branch"):
                touched.add((repo, "branch:" + str(item["head_branch"])))
        if not touched:
            return
        for e in entries:
            data = (e["cache"] or {}).get("data") or {}
            if not e["key"].startswith("pr:") or not data:
                continue
            _, owner, repo, _ = split_key(e["key"])
            marks = {"sha:" + str(data.get("headRefOid") or "").lower()}
            if data.get("headRefName"):
                marks.add("branch:" + str(data["headRefName"]))  # a push: runs for a head GitHub has not shown yet
            if any((f"{owner}/{repo}", m) in touched for m in marks):
                self.nudged.add(e["key"])

    def merge_comments(self, entries: list[dict], page: dict) -> None:
        """Add new, edited and deleted comments on watched issues to their cached comment list. The
        next GraphQL read (every COMMENT_CYCLE) replaces the list with GitHub's latest 50."""
        watched = {e["key"]: e for e in entries if e["key"].startswith("comment:")}
        for c in page.get("comments") or []:
            try:
                key = f"comment:{str(c.get('repo') or '').lower()}#{int(c.get('number') or 0)}"
            except (TypeError, ValueError):
                continue
            e = watched.get(key)
            if e is None:
                continue
            prior = e["cache"] or {}
            data = dict(prior.get("data") or {"url": None, "state": None, "type": None, "total": None, "comments": []})
            by_id = {x.get("id"): x for x in data.get("comments") or []}
            if c.get("deleted"):
                by_id.pop(c.get("id"), None)
            else:
                by_id[c.get("id")] = {"id": c.get("id"), "url": c.get("url"), "createdAt": c.get("created_at"),
                                      "author": c.get("author"), "body": str(c.get("body") or "")[:COMMENT_BODY_MAX]}
            data["comments"] = sorted(by_id.values(), key=lambda x: x.get("id") or 0)[-2 * COMMENTS_LAST:]
            self.store(key, e["cache"], data=data, dataAt=self.now, error=None)
            e["cache"] = read_cache(key)

    def prune(self, live: set[str]) -> None:
        if not cache_dir().is_dir():
            return
        names = {file_name(k) for k in live}
        for path in cache_dir().glob("*.json"):
            if path.name in names:
                continue
            try:
                if self.now - path.stat().st_mtime > CACHE_KEEP:
                    path.unlink()
            except OSError:
                pass


def log(event: dict) -> None:
    print(json.dumps({"at": dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds"), **event}), flush=True)


def clear_failing() -> None:
    """Drop the previous process's "failing" record. A restart or a re-exec (which is how a fix
    arrives) must not leave clients exiting 3 on it until the first good tick, which can take minutes."""
    status = read_json(BASE / "daemon.json")
    if isinstance(status, dict) and "failing" in status:
        status.pop("failing")
        write_json(BASE / "daemon.json", status)


class Watchdog:
    """Exits the process (EXIT_WATCHDOG) once the serve loop has not come round for `limit` seconds,
    so launchd (KeepAlive) or systemd (Restart=always) starts a fresh daemon. A thread, so it fires
    while the loop is stuck where no deadline reaches: a name lookup, a full disk, a bug. Before it
    exits it prints where every thread was (file, line, function; no values) to stderr."""

    def __init__(self, limit: float, clock: Callable[[], float] = time.monotonic,
                 exit: Callable[[int], Any] = os._exit, backstop: float | None = None) -> None:
        self.limit, self.clock, self.exit, self.backstop = limit, clock, exit, backstop
        self.last = clock()

    def beat(self) -> None:
        self.last = self.clock()
        if self.backstop is not None:  # re-arming cancels the previous timer
            faulthandler.dump_traceback_later(self.limit + self.backstop, exit=True)

    def check(self) -> bool:
        """True (after calling exit) when the loop is overdue."""
        lag = self.clock() - self.last
        if lag <= self.limit:
            return False
        log({"event": "watchdog", "silentFor": int(lag), "limit": int(self.limit), "pid": os.getpid()})
        with contextlib.suppress(Exception):  # stderr may not be a real file (tests)
            print(f"glaeda-gh: watchdog: the serve loop has not come round for {int(lag)}s (limit {int(self.limit)}s); "
                  f"exiting {EXIT_WATCHDOG} so the service manager restarts the daemon. Where each thread was:",
                  file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
            faulthandler.dump_traceback(file=sys.stderr, all_threads=True)
        self.exit(EXIT_WATCHDOG)
        return True

    def start(self) -> threading.Thread:
        def run() -> None:
            # hard_deadline's SIGALRM is meant for the main thread's blocking read. A process-directed
            # signal goes to any thread that does not block it, and taken here it interrupts nothing.
            if hasattr(signal, "pthread_sigmask"):
                signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, {signal.SIGALRM})
            while not self.check():
                time.sleep(max(0.05, min(15.0, self.limit / 10)))

        thread = threading.Thread(target=run, name="glaeda-gh-watchdog", daemon=True)
        thread.start()
        self.beat()
        return thread


def try_lock(lock: Any) -> bool:
    try:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        return True
    except BlockingIOError:
        return False


def record_holder(lock: Any) -> None:
    """Name this process in daemon.lock, so a waiting daemon can tell whom to stop when it hangs."""
    lock.seek(0)
    lock.truncate()
    lock.write(json.dumps({"pid": os.getpid(), "since": time.time()}) + "\n")
    lock.flush()


def lock_holder(now: float | None = None) -> dict | None:
    """The recorded lock holder: {"pid", "silent"}, silent being the seconds since its last
    heartbeat (or since it took the lock, before its first). None without a record. A holder older
    than this record format writes none, so it is not known here."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    try:
        record = json.loads((BASE / "daemon.lock").read_text() or "null")
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    pid = record.get("pid") if isinstance(record, dict) else None
    since = record.get("since") if isinstance(record, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(pid, int) or isinstance(pid, bool) or pid <= 0 or not isinstance(since, (int, float)):
        return None
    status = read_json(BASE / "daemon.json")
    at = status.get("at") if isinstance(status, dict) and status.get("pid") == pid else None
    last = max(since, at) if isinstance(at, (int, float)) else since
    return {"pid": pid, "silent": now - last}


def is_serve(pid: int) -> bool:
    """Whether `pid` is a live glaeda-gh serve other than this process (not a reused pid)."""
    if pid == os.getpid():
        return False
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
    except OSError:  # gone, or another user's
        return False
    try:
        result = run_bounded(["ps", "-ww", "-o", "command=", "-p", str(pid)], 10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return False
    words = result.stdout.split()
    return result.returncode == 0 and "serve" in words and any(Path(w).name == "glaeda-gh" for w in words)


def take_over(lock: Any, now: float | None = None) -> bool:
    """Stop a hung lock holder and take the lock. Only a recorded holder that is a live glaeda-gh
    serve and has been silent for more than STALE_HEARTBEAT is stopped: SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after
    TAKEOVER_GRACE. True once this process holds the lock."""
    holder = lock_holder(now)
    if holder is None or holder["silent"] <= STALE_HEARTBEAT or not is_serve(holder["pid"]):
        return False
    pid = holder["pid"]
    log({"event": "takeover", "pid": pid, "silentFor": int(holder["silent"]), "by": os.getpid()})
    for sig, grace in ((signal.SIGTERM, TAKEOVER_GRACE), (signal.SIGKILL, 5)):
        try:
            os.kill(pid, sig)
        except ProcessLookupError:
            pass
        deadline = time.monotonic() + grace
        while time.monotonic() < deadline:
            if try_lock(lock):
                return True
            time.sleep(0.2)
    return try_lock(lock)


def acquire_lock(lock: Any, sleep: Callable[[float], Any] = time.sleep) -> None:
    """Wait for the daemon lock (a second daemon waits instead of polling twice), taking it over
    from a holder whose heartbeat went stale."""
    waiting = False
    while not try_lock(lock):
        if not waiting:
            holder = lock_holder()
            log({"event": "waiting", "pid": os.getpid(), "holder": holder and holder["pid"]})
            waiting = True
        if take_over(lock):
            break
        sleep(LOCK_RETRY)
    record_holder(lock)


def serve(foreground_ok: bool = False) -> int:
    BASE.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True, mode=0o700)
    lock = open(BASE / "daemon.lock", "a+")
    acquire_lock(lock)
    clear_failing()
    me = Path(__file__).resolve()
    stamp = me.stat().st_mtime
    daemon = Daemon()
    log({"event": "start", "pid": os.getpid()})
    watchdog = Watchdog(WATCHDOG_AFTER, backstop=WATCHDOG_BACKSTOP)
    watchdog.start()
    last = None
    while True:
        try:
            status = daemon.tick()
            summary = (status["modes"], status["paths"], status["errors"])
            if summary != last and (status["errors"] or last is not None):
                log({"event": "state", "modes": status["modes"], "paths": status["paths"], "errors": status["errors"]})
            last = summary
        except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001  keep serving; only the type name is logged
            site = error_site(error)  # plus where it was raised
            log({"event": "error", "error": describe(error), "site": site})
            try:
                daemon.tick_failed(describe(error), site)
            except Exception as report:  # noqa: BLE001  e.g. a full disk: keep serving
                log({"event": "error", "error": describe(report), "site": "tick_failed"})
        time.sleep(TICK)
        watchdog.beat()
        try:
            if me.stat().st_mtime != stamp:
                log({"event": "reexec"})
                os.execv(sys.executable, [sys.executable, os.fspath(me), "serve"] + (["--foreground-ok"] if foreground_ok else []))
        except OSError:
            pass


SYSTEMD_UNIT = "glaeda-gh.service"


def under_service() -> bool:
    """Whether this process is the service manager's glaeda-gh (launchd names its job in
    XPC_SERVICE_NAME; systemd puts its unit in the process's cgroup)."""
    if os.environ.get("XPC_SERVICE_NAME") == LAUNCH_LABEL:
        return True
    try:
        return SYSTEMD_UNIT in Path("/proc/self/cgroup").read_text()
    except OSError:
        return False


def service_loaded() -> bool:
    """Whether this user's glaeda-gh service is loaded (launchd) or enabled or running (systemd)."""
    if sys.platform == "darwin":
        # the LaunchAgent, or the same label as a system LaunchDaemon on a headless Mac
        probes = [["launchctl", "print", f"gui/{os.getuid()}/{LAUNCH_LABEL}"],
                  ["launchctl", "print", f"system/{LAUNCH_LABEL}"]]
    else:
        probes = [["systemctl", "--user", "--quiet", "is-enabled", SYSTEMD_UNIT],
                  ["systemctl", "--user", "--quiet", "is-active", SYSTEMD_UNIT]]
    for probe in probes:
        try:
            if run_bounded(probe, 10).returncode == 0:
                return True
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            continue
    return False


def serve_refusal() -> str:
    where = (f"the LaunchAgent {LAUNCH_LABEL} is loaded" if sys.platform == "darwin"
             else f"the systemd user unit {SYSTEMD_UNIT} is enabled")
    return (f"serve: refusing to start: {where}, and it runs the daemon. A serve started from a shell only "
            "waits for its lock, or takes it and stops every session's waits when it hangs or its shell goes "
            "away (10-20). Do not run serve yourself. If the daemon is down or hung: "
            f"{start_hint(restart=True)}. To run one in the foreground anyway (debugging): "
            "glaeda-gh serve --foreground-ok")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- clients


def daemon_running(grace: float = 0.0) -> bool:
    """True while some process holds the daemon lock. With grace, keep looking that long: the lock is
    released for a moment while the daemon re-executes itself after an update."""
    deadline = time.monotonic() + grace
    while True:
        if lock_held():
            return True
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            return False
        time.sleep(0.25)


def lock_held() -> bool:
    try:
        with open(BASE / "daemon.lock", "r") as lock:
            try:
                fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_SH | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            except BlockingIOError:
                return True
            fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
            return False
    except OSError:
        return False


LAUNCH_LABEL = "com.teamleaderleo.glaeda.gh-watch"


# launchctl's "5: Input/output error" (cosmic-gray, macOS 27, 10-20): bootstrap answers it whenever the
# service is already loaded (reproduced), so it says nothing about the service's health. kickstart
# answered it while launchd reported "spawn failed" for the job; unloading and loading it starts over.
LAUNCHCTL_ERROR_5 = (f"If launchctl answers \"5: Input/output error\": from bootstrap it means the service is "
                     f"already loaded (kickstart it instead); from kickstart, reload it: launchctl bootout "
                     f"gui/$(id -u)/{LAUNCH_LABEL}; launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) "
                     f"~/Library/LaunchAgents/{LAUNCH_LABEL}.plist. Never run glaeda-gh serve yourself")


def start_hint(restart: bool = False) -> str:
    """How to (re)start the daemon here, including when its service was never loaded."""
    if sys.platform == "darwin":
        if restart:
            return f"launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/{LAUNCH_LABEL}. {LAUNCHCTL_ERROR_5}"
        return (f"launchctl kickstart gui/$(id -u)/{LAUNCH_LABEL} (if launchctl cannot find the service, load it: "
                f"launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/{LAUNCH_LABEL}.plist). "
                f"{LAUNCHCTL_ERROR_5}. Its log is ~/Library/Logs/glaeda-gh.err")
    if restart:
        return "systemctl --user restart glaeda-gh"
    return "systemctl --user start glaeda-gh (systemctl --user status glaeda-gh says why it stopped)"


DOWN = ("glaeda-gh: the daemon is not running, so nothing is polling GitHub; do not poll GitHub yourself "
        "instead. Start it: " + start_hint() + ". If it was never installed: glaeda-mini-setup --hygiene-only --apply.")


def daemon_stale() -> float | None:
    """Seconds since the daemon's last heartbeat, when that is so long ago that it looks hung."""
    status = read_json(BASE / "daemon.json")
    # an older daemon wrote daemon.json only when it fetched something: its silence means nothing
    at = status.get("at") if isinstance(status, dict) and status.get("heartbeat") else None
    if not isinstance(at, (int, float)):
        return None
    lag = time.time() - at
    return lag if lag > STALE_HEARTBEAT else None


def daemon_failing() -> dict | None:
    """The daemon's "failing" record, once every tick has raised for FAILING_AFTER seconds."""
    status = read_json(BASE / "daemon.json")
    broken = status.get("failing") if isinstance(status, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(broken, dict):
        return None
    at, since = status.get("at"), broken.get("since")
    if not isinstance(at, (int, float)) or not isinstance(since, (int, float)) or at - since < FAILING_AFTER:
        return None
    return broken


def failing_message(broken: dict) -> str:
    since = broken.get("since")
    when = dt.datetime.fromtimestamp(since, dt.timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%SZ") \
        if isinstance(since, (int, float)) else "?"
    where = f" at {broken['site']}" if broken.get("site") else ""
    return (f"glaeda-gh: the daemon is running, but every cycle since {when} has failed with "
            f"{broken.get('error') or '?'}{where} ({broken.get('count', '?')} times), so nothing is being "
            "refreshed. Restarting it will not fix this: the installed glaeda-gh needs a fix (scripts/glaeda-gh "
            "in teamleaderleo/glaeda). Tell the user; do not poll GitHub yourself in a loop meanwhile.")


def stale_message(lag: float) -> str:
    holder = lock_holder()
    who = ""
    if holder is not None and is_serve(holder["pid"]):
        # a holder outside the service is what a restart cannot reach: launchd restarts its own job,
        # which then waits behind the hung one (10-20)
        who = (f" The lock holder is pid {holder['pid']}; the service's waiting daemon stops it once it has been "
               f"silent for {STALE_HEARTBEAT}s, or stop it now: kill {holder['pid']}.")
    return (f"glaeda-gh: the daemon holds its lock but has not written a heartbeat for {int(lag)}s, so it "
            f"looks hung and nothing is being refreshed.{who} Restart it: {start_hint(restart=True)}")


def failing(checks: list[dict]) -> list[dict]:
    return [c for c in checks if (c.get("conclusion") or "").upper() in FAILED]


def cancelled_unstarted(c: dict) -> bool:
    """A PR check cancelled before any step ran: the rescue cancels a job still queued for a runner and
    re-runs it. Unknown steps (never read) count as started, so a real cancellation still fails."""
    return (c.get("conclusion") or "").upper() == "CANCELLED" and c.get("stepCount") == 0


def refused_check(c: dict) -> bool:
    """A PR check whose runner refused the job: its job-started hook step failed at job start."""
    return ((c.get("conclusion") or "").upper() == "FAILURE" and HOOK_STEP in (c.get("failedSteps") or [])
            and quick(c.get("startedAt"), c.get("completedAt")))


def refused_job(job: dict) -> bool:
    """A run's job that a glaeda runner refused (see refused_check); the runner is named, so check it."""
    glaeda = (GLAEDA_RUNNER.search(str(job.get("runner_name") or ""))
              or any(str(label).startswith("glaeda-") for label in job.get("labels") or []))
    return (job.get("conclusion") == "failure" and bool(glaeda) and HOOK_STEP in (job.get("failedSteps") or [])
            and quick(job.get("started_at"), job.get("completed_at")))


def classify(data: dict, now: float | None = None, grace: float = RESCUE_GRACE) -> dict[str, list[dict]]:
    """Split a PR's checks into passed, failed, pending and held. A failed check is held while a job
    of its workflow run was refused at runner setup less than `grace` seconds ago: the rescue re-runs
    that run's failed jobs, dependents such as an aggregate status check included. A check cancelled
    before any step ran is held as long: the rescue cancelled it in the queue and re-runs it."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    checks = data.get("checks") or []
    def recent(c: dict) -> bool:
        return now - (iso_epoch(c.get("completedAt")) or 0) < grace
    held_runs = {c.get("runId") for c in checks if c.get("runId") and refused_check(c) and recent(c)}
    # the rescue cancels the whole run: its jobs that were running come back cancelled with steps
    queued_runs = {c.get("runId") for c in checks if c.get("runId") and cancelled_unstarted(c) and recent(c)}
    out: dict[str, list[dict]] = {"passed": [], "failed": [], "pending": [], "held": []}
    for c in checks:
        cancelled = (c.get("conclusion") or "").upper() == "CANCELLED"
        if c.get("status") != "COMPLETED":
            out["pending"].append(c)
        elif (c.get("conclusion") or "").upper() in FAILED:
            held = (c.get("runId") in held_runs or (cancelled_unstarted(c) and recent(c))
                    or (cancelled and c.get("runId") in queued_runs))
            out["held" if held else "failed"].append(c)
        else:
            out["passed"].append(c)
    return out


def check_verdict(data: dict, until: str = "green", now: float | None = None,
                  grace: float = RESCUE_GRACE) -> int | None:
    """The checks alone, whatever the PR's state or head: EXIT_OK, EXIT_FAIL, or None while pending."""
    checks = data.get("checks") or []
    if data.get("checksTruncated"):
        # More than 100 checks: only the first 100 are here, so GitHub's rollup decides, except that a
        # check shown as held or still running defers its FAILURE (manaflow-ai/cmux#20733: a queued fast
        # tier the rescue cancelled read as a failure while 13 checks still ran).
        state = data.get("checkState")
        if state == "SUCCESS":
            return EXIT_OK
        if state not in {"FAILURE", "ERROR"}:
            return None
        groups = classify(data, now, grace)
        if until == "done" and (groups["pending"] or groups["held"]):
            return None  # every shown check finishes first, as under 100 checks
        if groups["failed"] and until != "required":
            return EXIT_FAIL
        return None if groups["held"] else EXIT_FAIL
    if not checks:
        return None  # nothing has reported yet
    marked = any(c.get("required") for c in checks)

    def decides(c: dict) -> bool:
        # Every check decides; only an explicit --until required ignores the ones GitHub does not require
        # (manaflow-ai/subrouter#612 merged red on "success" over two failed optional checks).
        return until != "required" or bool(c.get("required")) or not marked

    # GitHub's own merge state already counts a required check that never reported. A conflicted PR
    # (DIRTY) runs no pull_request workflows until the next push, and a merged one never will.
    waiting = (data.get("requiredMissing") and data.get("state") == "OPEN"
               and data.get("mergeStateStatus") not in {"CLEAN", "UNSTABLE", "HAS_HOOKS", "DIRTY"})
    groups = classify(data, now, grace)
    bad = [c for c in groups["failed"] if decides(c)]
    unfinished = [c for c in groups["pending"] + groups["held"] if decides(c)]
    if until in {"green", "required"} and bad:
        return EXIT_FAIL
    if unfinished or waiting:
        return None
    if data.get("requiredMissing") and data.get("state") == "OPEN" and data.get("mergeStateStatus") == "DIRTY":
        return EXIT_FAIL  # merge conflicts: the required checks will not run until the next push
    return EXIT_FAIL if bad else EXIT_OK


def run_hold(data: dict, jobs: list[dict] | None, now: float | None = None,
             grace: float = RESCUE_GRACE) -> list[dict]:
    """The jobs a glaeda runner refused, while a rescue attempt of this failed run may still come.
    Empty once any job failed for another reason, when the job list is not this attempt's, or
    `grace` seconds after the run finished."""
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    if (grace <= 0 or data.get("status") != "completed" or data.get("conclusion") not in {"failure", "cancelled"}
            or not jobs or jobs_attempt(jobs) < (data.get("run_attempt") or 1)):
        return []
    bad = [j for j in jobs if j.get("status") == "completed" and j.get("conclusion") not in RUN_OK]
    refused = [j for j in bad if refused_job(j)]
    # the rescue cancels what is still running before it re-runs, so cancelled jobs are expected
    if not refused or any(not refused_job(j) and j.get("conclusion") != "cancelled" for j in bad):
        return []
    finished = iso_epoch(data.get("updated_at")) or max(iso_epoch(j.get("completed_at")) or 0 for j in refused)
    return refused if now - finished < grace else []


def verdict(kind: str, data: dict | None, until: str = "green", sha: str | None = None,
            jobs: list[dict] | None = None, now: float | None = None, grace: float = RESCUE_GRACE) -> int | None:
    """EXIT_OK, EXIT_FAIL, EXIT_CONFLICT (a PR that conflicts with its base), or None while still pending."""
    if not data:
        return None
    if kind == "run":
        if sha and not str(data.get("head_sha") or "").startswith(sha):
            return None
        if data.get("status") != "completed":
            return None
        if data.get("conclusion") in RUN_OK:
            return EXIT_OK
        return None if run_hold(data, jobs, now, grace) else EXIT_FAIL
    if data.get("state") == "CLOSED":
        return EXIT_FAIL
    if until == "merged":
        return EXIT_OK if data.get("state") == "MERGED" else None
    if sha and not str(data.get("headRefOid") or "").startswith(sha):
        return None  # the push has not reached GitHub yet, or these are an older commit's checks
    if conflicting(data):
        return EXIT_CONFLICT  # no pull_request workflow runs until the next push
    return check_verdict(data, until, now, grace)


def conflicting(data: dict) -> bool:
    """An open PR GitHub says conflicts with its base. UNKNOWN is still being computed: not a conflict."""
    # mergeStateStatus can lag mergeable (or read DRAFT), so mergeable alone decides.
    return data.get("state") == "OPEN" and data.get("mergeable") == "CONFLICTING"


def age(seconds: float | None) -> str:
    if seconds is None:
        return "never"
    s = int(max(0, time.time() - seconds))
    return f"{s}s ago" if s < 120 else f"{s // 60}m ago"


def label(c: dict) -> str:
    return f"{c['workflow']} / {c.get('name')}" if c.get("workflow") else str(c.get("name"))


def steps_note(c: dict) -> str:
    names = c.get("failedSteps") or []
    if not names:
        return ""
    more = f" +{len(names) - 3}" if len(names) > 3 else ""
    return f" (step: {', '.join(names[:3])}{more})"


def outcome(conclusion: str | None) -> str:
    text = (conclusion or "").lower()
    return "failed" if text in {"failure", "error", ""} else text


def summary(key: str, entry: dict | None, until: str = "done", grace: float = RESCUE_GRACE) -> str:
    kind, owner, repo, number = split_key(key)
    name = f"{owner}/{repo}{'/' if kind == 'run' else '#'}{number}"
    if not entry:
        return f"{name}: no data yet"
    data = entry.get("data")
    lines = []
    if not data:
        lines.append(f"{name}: no data ({entry.get('error') or 'not fetched yet'})")
        return "\n".join(lines)
    now = time.time()
    if kind == "pr":
        head = (data.get("headRefOid") or "")[:10]
        draft = " draft" if data.get("isDraft") else ""
        lines.append(f"{name} {data.get('state')}{draft}  mergeable={data.get('mergeable')} "
                     f"({data.get('mergeStateStatus')})  review={data.get('reviewDecision')}  head {head}")
        checks = data.get("checks") or []
        groups = classify(data, now, grace)
        marked = any(c.get("required") for c in checks)
        more = "+" if data.get("checksTruncated") else ""
        if checks:
            # the headline is this tool's own reading, the one `wait` decides by; GitHub's rollup also
            # counts superseded attempts and checks that are not required
            result = check_verdict(data, "done" if until == "merged" else until, now, grace)
            state = {EXIT_OK: "SUCCESS", EXIT_FAIL: "FAILURE"}.get(result, "PENDING")
            held = f", {len(groups['held'])} held" if groups["held"] else ""
            rollup = data.get("checkState")
            differs = f"; GitHub's rollup says {rollup}" if rollup and rollup != state else ""
            lines.append(f"checks {state}: {len(groups['passed'])} passed, {len(groups['failed'])} failed, "
                         f"{len(groups['pending'])} pending{held} (of {len(checks)}{more}, "
                         f"{sum(1 for c in checks if c.get('required'))} required){differs}")
        else:
            lines.append("checks: none reported yet for this head commit")
        if data.get("requiredMissing"):
            why = " (merge conflicts: they run after the next push)" if data.get("mergeStateStatus") == "DIRTY" else ""
            lines.append(f"  required, not reported yet: {', '.join(data['requiredMissing'])}{why}")
        bad = groups["failed"]
        for c in bad[:20]:
            optional = " (not required)" if marked and not c.get("required") else ""
            lines.append(f"  {outcome(c.get('conclusion'))}: {label(c)}{steps_note(c)}{optional}  {c.get('url') or ''}".rstrip())
        if len(bad) > 20:
            lines.append(f"  ... {len(bad) - 20} more failed")
        for c in groups["held"][:20]:
            refused = any(refused_check(o) and o.get("runId") == c.get("runId") for o in checks)
            why = ("cancelled before any step ran" if cancelled_unstarted(c)
                   else "its run had a job refused at runner setup" if refused
                   else "its run was cancelled while a job was still queued")
            lines.append(f"  held: {label(c)}{steps_note(c)}: {why}; "
                         f"pending up to {int(grace)}s for the re-run  {c.get('url') or ''}".rstrip())
        for c in (data.get("superseded") or [])[:10]:
            optional = " (not required)" if marked and not c.get("required") else ""
            lines.append(f"  superseded: {label(c)} {outcome(c.get('conclusion'))}{optional}, "
                         "replaced by a newer attempt or run (not counted)")
        lines.append(str(data.get("url") or ""))
    elif kind == "comment":
        lines.append(f"{name} {str(data.get('type') or '').lower()} {data.get('state')}: "
                     f"{data.get('total')} comments  {data.get('url') or ''}".rstrip())
        for c in (data.get("comments") or [])[-5:]:
            first = (str(c.get("body") or "").strip().splitlines() or [""])[0][:100]
            lines.append(f"  {c.get('id')} {c.get('author')} {c.get('createdAt')}: {first}")
    else:
        lines.append(f"{name} {data.get('name')}: {data.get('status')}"
                     f"{' / ' + str(data.get('conclusion')) if data.get('conclusion') else ''}"
                     f"  attempt {data.get('run_attempt')}  {data.get('head_branch')} {str(data.get('head_sha') or '')[:10]}")
        jobs = entry.get("jobs")
        if jobs is not None:
            bad = [j for j in jobs if j.get("conclusion") not in (None, *RUN_OK)]
            live = [j for j in jobs if j.get("status") != "completed"]
            covered = jobs_attempt(jobs)
            stale = (f" (from attempt {covered}; attempt {data.get('run_attempt')} not read yet)"
                     if 0 < covered < (data.get("run_attempt") or 1) else "")
            lines.append(f"jobs: {len(jobs)} total, {len(bad)} failed, {len(live)} not finished{stale}")
            for j in bad[:20]:
                refused = f" (refused by {j.get('runner_name')})" if refused_job(j) else ""
                lines.append(f"  {outcome(j.get('conclusion'))}: {j.get('name')}{steps_note(j)}{refused}  "
                             f"{j.get('html_url') or ''}".rstrip())
        held = run_hold(data, jobs, now, grace)
        if held:
            lines.append(f"  held: attempt {data.get('run_attempt')} failed only because a glaeda runner refused "
                         f"{', '.join(str(j.get('name')) for j in held[:5])} at setup; pending up to {int(grace)}s "
                         "for a rescue attempt")
        lines.append(str(data.get("html_url") or ""))
    note = f"  (last error: {entry['error']})" if entry.get("error") else ""
    lines.append(f"data {age(entry.get('dataAt'))}{note}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def emit(key: str, entry: dict | None, as_json: bool, extra: dict | None = None, until: str = "done",
         grace: float = RESCUE_GRACE) -> None:
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps({"key": key, **(entry or {}), **(extra or {})}, sort_keys=True))
    else:
        print(summary(key, entry, until, grace))
        for k, v in (extra or {}).items():
            print(f"{k}: {v}")


def cmd_status(kind: str, target: str, jobs: bool, as_json: bool, first_wait: float = CYCLE + 10) -> int:
    key = parse_key(kind, target)
    if not daemon_running(grace=DOWN_GRACE):
        print(DOWN, file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_DOWN
    broken = daemon_failing()
    if broken is not None:
        print(failing_message(broken), file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_DOWN
    lag = daemon_stale()
    if lag is not None:
        print(stale_message(lag), file=sys.stderr)
    register(key, jobs)
    deadline = time.monotonic() + first_wait
    entry = read_cache(key)
    while (entry is None or (jobs and "jobs" not in entry and not entry.get("error"))) and time.monotonic() < deadline:
        time.sleep(1)
        entry = read_cache(key)
    emit(key, entry, as_json)
    return EXIT_OK if entry and entry.get("data") else EXIT_FAIL


def git_rev_parse(prefix: str) -> str | None:
    """The full id of `prefix` in the current checkout, if it names exactly one commit. Local only."""
    try:
        result = run_bounded(["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{prefix}^{{commit}}"], 5)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    full = result.stdout.strip().lower()
    return full if result.returncode == 0 and re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{40}", full) and full.startswith(prefix) else None


def resolve_sha(sha: str) -> tuple[str, bool]:
    """(commit, exact). A 40-hex id is exact. A prefix is resolved in the current checkout; one that
    does not resolve stays a prefix, which must then match the head GitHub shows (see cmd_wait)."""
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-fA-F]{7,40}", sha):
        raise WatchError(f"--sha must be a commit id (git rev-parse HEAD), 40 hex digits or a prefix of at least 7, not {sha!r}")
    sha = sha.lower()
    if len(sha) == 40:
        return sha, True
    full = git_rev_parse(sha)
    return (full, True) if full else (sha, False)


def cmd_wait(kind: str, target: str, until: str, timeout: float, jobs: bool, as_json: bool,
             sha: str | None = None, grace: float = RESCUE_GRACE) -> int:
    """Rules only on data the daemon fetched after this wait began, never on an older cache."""
    key = parse_key(kind, target)
    exact = True
    if sha:
        sha, exact = resolve_sha(sha)
    began, start = time.time(), time.monotonic()
    touched = -TOUCH_EVERY
    warned = False
    while True:
        if not daemon_running(grace=DOWN_GRACE):
            print(DOWN, file=sys.stderr)
            return EXIT_DOWN
        broken = daemon_failing()
        if broken is not None:
            print(failing_message(broken), file=sys.stderr)
            return EXIT_DOWN
        lag = daemon_stale()
        if lag is not None and not warned:
            print(stale_message(lag), file=sys.stderr)
            warned = True
        now = time.monotonic()
        if now - touched >= TOUCH_EVERY:
            register(key, jobs)
            touched = now
        entry = read_cache(key)
        fresh = entry is not None and (entry.get("fetchedAt") or 0) >= began
        waited = int(now - start)
        if fresh and not entry.get("data") and entry.get("missing"):
            emit(key, entry, as_json, {"result": "failure", "waited_s": waited}, until, grace)
            return EXIT_FAIL  # GitHub says NOT_FOUND; transport errors and 5xx stay pending
        data = (entry or {}).get("data")
        # only a merge is final; a completed run can be rerun and a closed PR reopened
        final = kind == "pr" and data and data.get("state") == "MERGED"
        usable = data and ((entry.get("dataAt") or 0) >= began or final)
        if usable and sha:
            head = str(data.get("headRefOid" if kind == "pr" else "head_sha") or "").lower()
            if head and not head.startswith(sha):
                if kind == "run":
                    raise WatchError(f"run {target} is for commit {head[:12]}, not {sha[:12]}; a run's commit never changes")
                if not exact:
                    raise WatchError(f"--sha {sha} is not a commit in this checkout and is not the head GitHub shows "
                                     f"({head[:12]}); pass the full id (git rev-parse HEAD) to wait for a push "
                                     "GitHub has not seen yet")
            elif head and not exact:
                sha, exact = head, True  # resolved: a later push now counts as pending, as for a full id
        result = verdict(kind, data, until, sha, (entry or {}).get("jobs"), time.time(), grace) if usable else None
        if result is not None:
            outcome_name = {EXIT_OK: "success", EXIT_CONFLICT: "conflict"}.get(result, "failure")
            extra: dict[str, Any] = {"result": outcome_name, "waited_s": waited}
            if kind == "pr" and result == EXIT_CONFLICT:
                extra["note"] = ("PR conflicts with its base (mergeable CONFLICTING): no checks run until you "
                                 "merge or rebase onto the base and push")
            elif kind == "pr" and data.get("state") == "CLOSED":
                extra["note"] = "PR was closed without being merged"
            if kind == "run" and result == EXIT_FAIL and grace > 0 and run_hold(data, entry.get("jobs"), time.time(), float("inf")):
                extra["note"] = f"a glaeda runner refused this attempt at setup and no rescue attempt followed within {int(grace)}s"
            emit(key, entry, as_json, extra, until, grace)
            return result
        if now - start >= timeout:
            extra = {"result": "timeout", "waited_s": waited}
            if sha and entry and entry.get("data"):
                head = entry["data"].get("headRefOid") or entry["data"].get("head_sha") or ""
                if not str(head).startswith(sha):
                    extra["note"] = f"GitHub still shows head {str(head)[:10]}, not {sha[:10]}"
            emit(key, entry, as_json, extra, until, grace)
            return EXIT_TIMEOUT
        time.sleep(min(WAIT_POLL, max(0.1, timeout - (now - start))))


def parse_since(text: str | None, began: float) -> tuple[int | None, float | None]:
    """(comment id, epoch): a comment id or URL, an ISO time, or a duration back from now (10m)."""
    if not text:
        return None, began
    text = text.strip()
    match = re.fullmatch(r"(?:https://github\.com/\S*#issuecomment-)?(\d+)", text)
    if match:
        return int(match.group(1)), None
    match = re.fullmatch(r"(\d+)([smhd])", text)
    if match:
        return None, began - int(match.group(1)) * {"s": 1, "m": 60, "h": 3600, "d": 86400}[match.group(2)]
    at = iso_epoch(text) if re.match(r"\d{4}-\d\d-\d\d", text) else None
    if at is None:
        raise WatchError(f"--since must be a comment id or URL, an ISO time (2026-09-25T18:00:00Z) or a "
                         f"duration such as 10m, not {text!r}")
    return None, at


def login(name: str | None) -> str:
    """GraphQL names the Actions bot `github-actions`, REST and the web `github-actions[bot]`."""
    return re.sub(r"\[bot\]$", "", str(name or "").strip().lower().removeprefix("app/"))


def comment_matches(c: dict, author: str | None, pattern: re.Pattern | None, since_id: int | None,
                    since_time: float | None) -> bool:
    if since_id is not None and (c.get("id") or 0) <= since_id:
        return False
    if since_time is not None and (iso_epoch(c.get("createdAt")) or 0) < int(since_time):
        return False
    if author and login(c.get("author")) != login(author):
        return False
    return not pattern or bool(pattern.search(str(c.get("body") or "")))


def cmd_wait_comment(target: str, timeout: float, as_json: bool, author: str | None = None,
                     match: str | None = None, since: str | None = None) -> int:
    """Wait for a comment on an issue or PR by `author` whose body matches `match`, posted after
    `since` (by default after this wait began). Exits 0 with the comment, 1 when the issue does not
    exist, 2 on timeout."""
    key = parse_key("comment", target)
    try:
        pattern = re.compile(match) if match else None
    except re.error as error:
        raise WatchError(f"--match is not a regular expression: {error}") from None
    began, start = time.time(), time.monotonic()
    since_id, since_time = parse_since(since, began)
    touched = -TOUCH_EVERY
    warned = False
    _, owner, repo, number = split_key(key)
    name = f"{owner}/{repo}#{number}"
    while True:
        if not daemon_running(grace=DOWN_GRACE):
            print(DOWN, file=sys.stderr)
            return EXIT_DOWN
        broken = daemon_failing()
        if broken is not None:
            print(failing_message(broken), file=sys.stderr)
            return EXIT_DOWN
        lag = daemon_stale()
        if lag is not None and not warned:
            print(stale_message(lag), file=sys.stderr)
            warned = True
        now = time.monotonic()
        if now - touched >= TOUCH_EVERY:
            register(key)
            touched = now
        entry = read_cache(key)
        waited = int(now - start)
        if entry and (entry.get("fetchedAt") or 0) >= began and not entry.get("data") and entry.get("missing"):
            emit(key, entry, as_json, {"result": "failure", "waited_s": waited})
            return EXIT_FAIL
        comments = ((entry or {}).get("data") or {}).get("comments") or []
        found = next((c for c in comments if comment_matches(c, author, pattern, since_id, since_time)), None)
        if found:
            if as_json:
                print(json.dumps({"key": key, "result": "success", "waited_s": waited, "comment": found,
                                  "url": (entry.get("data") or {}).get("url")}, sort_keys=True))
            else:
                body = str(found.get("body") or "").splitlines()
                print(f"{name}: comment {found.get('id')} by {found.get('author')} at {found.get('createdAt')}")
                print(found.get("url") or "")
                for line in body[:40]:
                    print(f"  {line}")
                if len(body) > 40:
                    print(f"  ... {len(body) - 40} more lines")
                print(f"result: success\nwaited_s: {waited}")
            return EXIT_OK
        if now - start >= timeout:
            newer = sum(1 for c in comments if comment_matches(c, None, None, since_id, since_time))
            where = "since the wait began" if not since else f"after {since}"
            hint = "; to include a reply posted before the wait, pass --since with your own comment's URL" if not since else ""
            emit(key, entry, as_json, {"result": "timeout", "waited_s": waited,
                                       "note": f"{newer} comments {where}, none matched{hint}"})
            return EXIT_TIMEOUT
        time.sleep(min(WAIT_POLL, max(0.1, timeout - (now - start))))


def controller_line(ctl: dict) -> str:
    state = ctl.get("state")
    scope = "" if ctl.get("token") else ", public repos only (no token)"
    if state == "ok":
        return (f"controller: webhook events ok, {ctl.get('runs', 0)} watched runs from it, last delivery "
                f"{age(iso_epoch(ctl.get('lastDelivery')))}{scope}")
    if state in {"down", "absent"}:
        why = "no run-events receiver there" if state == "absent" else f"unreachable ({ctl.get('error')})"
        return f"controller: {why}; polling GitHub meanwhile, next try in {max(0, int((ctl.get('retryAt') or 0) - time.time()))}s"
    if state == "off":
        return "controller: none configured; polling GitHub"
    return "controller: starting"


def cmd_budget(as_json: bool) -> int:
    running = daemon_running(grace=DOWN_GRACE)
    budget = read_json(BASE / "budget.json") or {}
    status = read_json(BASE / "daemon.json") or {}
    lag = daemon_stale() if running else None
    broken = daemon_failing() if running else None
    watching = len(list(watch_dir().glob("*.json"))) if watch_dir().is_dir() else 0
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps({"daemon": running, "stale": lag is not None, "failing": broken is not None,
                          "budget": budget, "watching": watching, "last": status}, sort_keys=True))
    else:
        print(f"daemon: {'running' if running else 'NOT RUNNING'}, watching {watching}, "
              f"last heartbeat {age(status.get('at'))}")
        for bucket in ("rest", "graphql"):
            b = budget.get(bucket)
            if not b:
                print(f"{bucket:<8} not seen yet")
                continue
            reset = b.get("reset")
            when = dt.datetime.fromtimestamp(reset, dt.timezone.utc).strftime("%H:%M:%SZ") if reset else "?"
            rate = (status.get("perMinute") or {}).get(bucket)
            spend = f", {rate}/min to spend until then" if isinstance(rate, (int, float)) and rate >= 0 else ""
            print(f"{bucket:<8} {b.get('remaining')}/{b.get('limit')} left, resets {when}{spend}, seen {age(b.get('seenAt'))}")
        paths, reads = status.get("paths"), status.get("reads")
        if isinstance(paths, dict):
            name = {"graphql": "GraphQL", "rest": "REST"}
            print("reading: " + ", ".join(f"{label} over {name.get(paths.get(kind), '?')}" for kind, label in
                                          (("pr", "PRs"), ("run", "runs"), ("comment", "comments")))
                  + " (each goes to the richer bucket for the time left until its reset)")
            if isinstance(reads, dict):
                print("reads in the last hour: " + "; ".join(
                    f"{label} {(reads.get(kind) or {}).get('graphql', 0)} GraphQL / {(reads.get(kind) or {}).get('rest', 0)} REST"
                    for kind, label in (("pr", "PRs"), ("run", "runs"), ("comment", "comments"))))
        oldest = status.get("oldestData")
        if isinstance(oldest, dict):
            def span(v: Any) -> str:
                return "-" if not isinstance(v, (int, float)) else f"{int(v)}s" if v < 120 else f"{int(v // 60)}m"
            batch = status.get("batch") or {}
            print("oldest data of an open watch: " + ", ".join(
                f"{label} {span(oldest.get(kind))}" for kind, label in (("pr", "PRs"), ("run", "runs"), ("comment", "comments")))
                  + f" (GraphQL batch: {batch.get('pr', '?')} PRs, {batch.get('run', '?')} runs, "
                    f"{batch.get('comment', '?')} comment watches per query)")
        ctl = status.get("controller")
        if isinstance(ctl, dict):
            print(controller_line(ctl))
        hour = status.get("lastHour")
        if isinstance(hour, dict):
            print(f"daemon requests in the last hour: {hour.get('rest', 0)} REST, {hour.get('rest304', 0)} more "
                  f"REST answered 304 (free), {hour.get('graphql', 0)} GraphQL")
        if status.get("errors"):
            print("last errors: " + "; ".join(status["errors"][:3]))
    if not running:
        print(DOWN, file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_DOWN
    if broken is not None:
        print(failing_message(broken), file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_DOWN
    if lag is not None:
        print(stale_message(lag), file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_DOWN
    return EXIT_OK


def parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="glaeda-gh", description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest="command", required=True)
    sv = sub.add_parser("serve", help="run the daemon (the only process that polls GitHub); launchd or systemd runs it")
    sv.add_argument("--foreground-ok", action="store_true",
                    help="start even though the LaunchAgent or systemd unit is loaded (debugging only)")
    for name in ("status", "wait"):
        s = sub.add_parser(name)
        s.add_argument("kind", choices=("pr", "run", "comment"))
        s.add_argument("target", help="OWNER/REPO#N, OWNER/REPO/RUN_ID, or a GitHub URL")
        s.add_argument("--jobs", action="store_true", help="runs: also track the job list")
        s.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
        if name == "wait":
            s.add_argument("--until", choices=("green", "done", "required", "merged"), default="green",
                           help="PRs: green (every check passes; fail fast), done (every check finished), "
                                "required (only the checks GitHub requires decide; fail fast) or merged; "
                                "all but merged exit 4 once the PR conflicts with its base")
            s.add_argument("--timeout", type=float, default=3600, help="seconds (default 3600); exit 2 after")
            s.add_argument("--sha", help="the commit you pushed (git rev-parse HEAD): other heads count as pending")
            s.add_argument("--rescue-grace", type=float, default=RESCUE_GRACE, metavar="S",
                           help=f"hold a failure caused only by a glaeda runner refusing a job at setup this long "
                                f"for a rescue re-run (default {RESCUE_GRACE}; 0 rules on each attempt)")
            s.add_argument("--author", help="comments: only this author (github-actions[bot] or github-actions)")
            s.add_argument("--match", help="comments: a regular expression the body must contain")
            s.add_argument("--since", help="comments: after this comment id or URL, ISO time, or duration "
                                           "(10m); default the moment the wait began")
    b = sub.add_parser("budget", help="REST and GraphQL quota as the daemon last saw it")
    b.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    return p


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    args = parser().parse_args(argv)
    try:
        if args.command == "serve":
            if not args.foreground_ok and not under_service() and service_loaded():
                raise WatchError(serve_refusal())
            return serve(args.foreground_ok)
        if args.command == "budget":
            return cmd_budget(args.json)
        if args.command == "status":
            return cmd_status(args.kind, args.target, args.jobs, args.json)
        if args.rescue_grace < 0:
            raise WatchError("--rescue-grace must be 0 or more seconds")
        if args.kind == "comment":
            if args.sha or args.jobs or args.until != "green":
                raise WatchError("wait comment takes --author, --match and --since, not --sha, --jobs or --until")
            return cmd_wait_comment(args.target, args.timeout, args.json, args.author, args.match, args.since)
        if args.author or args.match or args.since:
            raise WatchError("--author, --match and --since are for wait comment")
        return cmd_wait(args.kind, args.target, args.until, args.timeout, args.jobs, args.json, args.sha,
                        args.rescue_grace)
    except WatchError as error:
        print(f"glaeda-gh: {error}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 64
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        return 130


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
